\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A dystopian plot\u8217\'20\'19: how will Trump\u8217\'20\'19s Project 2025 affect California?\b0\par\par\uc2 Andrew Gumbel in Los Angeles \par \uc2 1,140 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 July 2024\par 18:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The plan takes specific aim at California on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and green policies \u8211\'20\'13 but the state has ways to fight it\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  has not been shy about attacking California on the presidential campaign trail, telling fellow conservatives that \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.c-span.org/video/?533737-1/president-trump-speaks-cpac"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the place is failing}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D under Democratic party leadership. And all signs suggest that a second Trump administration would not hesitate to take a sledgehammer to principles and policy priorities that the Golden state and other progressive bastions hold dear.\par \par \uc2 The Project 2025 policy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://static.project2025.org/2025_MandateForLeadership_FULL.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 document}}}\uc2 , a blueprint for a second Trump presidency drawn up by former Trump administration officials and sympathetic thinktank analysts, takes specific aim at California on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, fuel emissions standards and the transition to electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 The document also raises the possibility of a large-scale crackdown on immigration and an intense focus on border security \u8211\'20\'13 a cornerstone of the Trump campaign that could upend the lives of millions of immigrants living in California as well as parts of the state economy, especially agriculture, that depend heavily on immigrant labor.\par \par \uc2 That is not to mention the other ways Project 2025 envisions overhauling the \uc2 US government\uc2 , with implications for California as much as the rest of the country: enhancing the power of the presidency and eroding the independence of the justice department, dismantling what it calls \u8220\'20\'1Cthe administrative state\u8221\'20\'1D, abandoning efforts to combat the climate crisis and curbing the rights of LGBTQ+ Americans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CProject 2025 is more than an idea,\u8221\'20\'1D the California congressman \uc2 Jared Huffman\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://huffman.house.gov/media-center/press-releases/congressional-leaders-form-task-force-to-counter-project-2025-and-defend-democracy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 warned}}}\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s a dystopian plot that\u8217\'20\'19s already in motion to dismantle our democratic institutions, abolish checks and balances, chip away at church-state separation, and impose a far-right agenda that infringes on basic liberties and violates public will.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 What remains to be seen, though, is how much of the wishlist laid out in Project 2025\u8217\'20\'19s 900-page \u8220\'20\'1CMandate for Leadership\u8221\'20\'1D is actually achievable. Trump himself has sought to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jul/08/trump-project-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 distance himself from the document}}}\uc2 , as Democrats like Huffman have started using it as a cudgel with which to attack his campaign. And a number of policy experts at one remove from the heat and hyperbole of the election campaign believe that any dystopian plot might quickly give way to a lot of lawsuits likely to slow or halt parts of the Trump agenda for months or years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think they are capable of pulling off a lot of the things they want to pull off,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Christopher Thornberg\uc2  of the Los Angeles-based research and consulting firm \uc2 Beacon Economics\uc2 . While an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/03/trump-mass-deportations-detention-camps-military-migrants"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 immigration crackdown}}}\uc2  similar to the one in Trump\u8217\'20\'19s first term seems inevitable, mass expulsions of millions of people as promised by the former president would be dizzyingly expensive and near-impossible to pull off, he argued.\par \par \uc2 As for California\u8217\'20\'19s more ambitious environmental targets that Project 2025 wants to disrupt, some \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/24/california-ban-sales-gas-powered-cars-2035"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 getting rid of gas-powered cars by 2035}}}\uc2 , for example \u8211\'20\'13 are probably unfeasible.\par \par \uc2 On many other issues, California can draw on its experience of the first Trump presidency to throw up roadblocks or pass its own state legislation. The Project 2025 document may be a sign that Trump and his allies are more ready to govern this time, but \u8211\'20\'13 as the political consultant and co-founder of the anti-Trump Lincoln Project Mike Madrid argues \u8211\'20\'13 California and other blue states are better prepared, too.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhatever the federal government decides to do, California can compensate,\u8221\'20\'1D Madrid said. In the event of a second Trump presidency, he expected the state to start filing lawsuits almost immediately, as it did more than a hundred times during the 2017-21 presidency, and find administrative or legislative solutions to many of the problems a new Trump administration might try to create.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis state is good at finding ways around the policies,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe size of the economy makes it easier to do that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In one instance \u8211\'20\'13 a proposal in the Project 2025 document to end a legal waiver that has enabled California to set its own fuel efficiency standards for the past half-century \u8211\'20\'13 the courts have already heard a suit brought by several Republican-run states and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.courthousenews.com/dc-circuit-affirms-california-waiver-for-strict-emissions-standards/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled}}}\uc2  in California\u8217\'20\'19s favor.\par \par \uc2 None of that diminishes the threats that Trump and his supporters have been directing at California\u8217\'20\'19s political leadership, or the nastiness of some of their language. In speeches over the past year, Trump has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/politics/story/2024-03-18/column-trump-has-big-plans-for-california-in-the-second-term-hes-seeking-fasten-your-seatbelts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mischaracterized}}}\uc2  California as a place with so little water that even rich people in Beverly Hills can\u8217\'20\'19t take proper showers, a place where shoplifting and other crimes are so rampant the only solution is to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/30/donald-trump-california-speech-robbers-shot"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shoot criminals on sight}}}\uc2 , a place where undocumented immigrants are, implausibly, offered pension funds and mansions on arrival and can vote illegally multiple times over. \u8220\'20\'1CThe world is being dumped into California,\u8221\'20\'1D he told state Republicans last September. \u8220\'20\'1CPrisoners. Terrorists. Mental patients.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Project 2025\u8217\'20\'19s approach has been less fanciful and much more focused on policy detail. It rails, for example, against what it calls \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  tourism\u8221\'20\'1D in California and other states and proposes a number of administrative remedies to track women who travel there because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in their states, and to withhold Medicaid and other federal funding if California continues to insist that insurance companies make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  part of their health coverage.\par \par \uc2 None of this, though, is as frightening to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists in California as the part that is left unsaid: the desire of many on the political right to institute an outright national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Asked whether she believed Trump when he said he would not support such a ban, Jodi Hicks of the Planned Parenthood Affiliates of California responded with a flat \u8220\'20\'1CNo.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 To her, the fight in California is not about the fine print of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aclu.org/documents/access-denied-origins-hyde-amendment-and-other-restrictions-public-funding-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hyde amendment}}}\uc2  or the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nwlc.org/resource/the-weldon-amendment-a-poison-pill-rider-for-abortion-access/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Weldon amendement}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 tools invoked by Project 2025 that Republicans have used in the past to try to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the country \u8211\'20\'13 but rather about control of Congress to avert even the possibility of a national ban.\par \par \uc2 Hicks has identified {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthoodaction.org/planned-parenthood-affiliates-california/media/planned-parenthood-affiliates-of-ca-votes-pac-launches-seven-fig"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eight swing districts}}}\uc2  in California that she believes can determine control of the House of Representatives and her organization is spending hundreds of thousands of dollars to support the Democratic candidates there. \u8220\'20\'1CThe road to reproductive freedom runs through California,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe know what the intention is \u8211\'20\'13 they want to take away \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights all across the country, including California. What we need is a Congress that can push back and protect us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The best way to thwart the Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 plans, in other words, is to vote against them. California, as a solid-blue state, will do its part to keep Trump out of the White House. What the rest of the country does remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240717ek7h001bc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
