\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Arkansas official rejected valid  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ballot signatures, lawsuit claims\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 418 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 July 2024\par 20:33 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Group seeking to enshrine rights in state constitution sue secretary of state over \u8216\'20\'18refusal to count valid signatures\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Organizers behind a ballot measure to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the Arkansas state constitution sued a senior state official on Tuesday, accusing him of illegally rejecting the signatures they submitted in support of putting the measure on the November ballot.\par \par \uc2 The group, Arkansans for Limited Government, submitted more than 101,000 signatures backing its ballot measure on 5 July, according to its lawsuit. Five days later, the Arkansas secretary of state John Thurston rejected their signatures because, he said, they failed to turn in the required paperwork, including a statement that identified any paid canvassers used by the group.\par \par \uc2 In its lawsuit, Arkansans for Limited Government fired back, claiming that the group had fully complied with Arkansas law and submitted canvassers\u8217\'20\'19 names. They also argued that even if they had not complied with the law, they should be given the chance to correct the paperwork.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur compliance with the law is clear and well-documented,\u8221\'20\'1D Lauren Cowles, executive director of Arkansans for Limited Government, said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CThe secretary of state\u8217\'20\'19s refusal to count valid signatures is an affront to democracy and an attempt to undermine the will of the people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Arkansas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2023/nov/10/state-abortion-laws-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 currently bans all abortions}}}\uc2  except in medical emergencies. Arkansans for Limited Government\u8217\'20\'19s ballot measure would permit people to get abortions up until 18 weeks of pregnancy, as well as in cases of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are reviewing the lawsuit and would have no further comment at this time,\u8221\'20\'1D Chris Powell, press secretary for the Arkansas secretary of state, said in an email.\par \par \uc2 In order to go before voters in November, the ballot measure must be certified by 22 August. Arkansans for Limited Government\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit asks the Arkansas supreme court to force Thurston to start counting and verifying signatures so that the measure can meet that deadline.\par \par \uc2 In the two years since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, voters in states like Michigan, Ohio and Kansas have all passed ballot measures to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. A number of states, including Nevada, Arizona and Florida are slated to put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ballot measures before voters this November. Democrats are hoping that these measures will boost turnout among their base, while anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists and their Republican allies have tried to squash similar measures in states like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://19thnews.org/2024/05/missouri-abortion-ballot-measure-signatures/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Missouri}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jun/18/abortion-rights-south-dakota-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 South Dakota}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240716ek7g00335\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
FF
