\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 What would a Harris candidacy mean for the elusive independent woman voter?\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,037 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2024\par 10:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Polling shows critical group unenthused about Biden and Trump \u8211\'20\'13 and moved by economy over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The independent voter is the elusive prize in any election. This year, Democrats are hoping that widespread support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will draw in women who might have previously shied away from the party.\par \par \uc2 But polling shows that independent female voters, a critical group for any candidate, are particularly unenthused about the prospect of choosing between the same two guys who ran for election last time. Now, with some Democrats publicly mulling replacing Joe Biden in the wake of his catastrophic debate performance and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  emerging as a possible heir apparent, a key question is whether a Harris-topped ticket would provide them with some much-needed excitement .\par \par \uc2 Over the last two decades, the share of registered voters who tell pollsters that they identify as \u8220\'20\'1Cindependent\u8221\'20\'1D has crept up to roughly 30% of the electorate, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2024/04/09/the-partisanship-and-ideology-of-american-voters/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to April data from the Pew Research Center}}}\uc2 . But that number masks a basic divide among independents: although they are often thought of as centrists who flop between Republicans and Democrats, Pew found that all but 3% of self-identified independents lean conservative or liberal.\par \par \uc2 These individuals, experts say, consistently vote for one party but choose to call themselves independents because they distrust the party apparatus. Rather than a rebuke of US political polarization, the growing number of self-identified independents may be a symptom of it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe independent piece of the electorate is much harder to pin down, because this is the group to which people go when they\u8217\'20\'19re dissatisfied with the two major parties,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kelly Dittmar, director of research at the Center for American Women and Politics at Rutgers\u8217\'20\'19 Eagleton Institute of Politics. \u8220\'20\'1CThat dissatisfaction can come from a lot of directions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Whether closet partisans or true swing votes, this group largely remains a mystery even to experts. Independent women in particular don\u8217\'20\'19t tend to have much in common demographically, which makes their voting patterns hard to predict, Dittmar said.\par \par \uc2 Still, there are some key voting demographics that lean independent. Hispanic women make up a growing share of the electorate \u8211\'20\'13 Hispanic Americans will account for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2024/01/10/key-facts-about-hispanic-eligible-voters-in-2024/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a record 14.7% of eligible voters}}}\uc2  in November \u8211\'20\'13 and they are increasingly identifying as independent, according to Eduardo Gamarra, who heads the Latino Public Opinion Forum at \uc2 Florida International University\uc2 . Like the rest of the country, they\u8217\'20\'19ve become more and more frustrated with US politics.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COver the last decade, they trusted American institutions more than [other Americans],\u8221\'20\'1D Gamarra said. \u8220\'20\'1CNow, we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing that Hispanics are essentially the same [as other demographics]. They don\u8217\'20\'19t trust parties. They don\u8217\'20\'19t trust politicians. But this is especially accentuated with Biden and the Democrats.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This could be a particular problem for Biden in Arizona, a major battleground state. A quarter of Hispanic female voters there say they are independent, while another quarter identify as Republicans and half as Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Harris has better a favorability rating among Hispanic and Black voters than Biden, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2024/06/12/kamala-harris-favorability-poll-00162093"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to recent polling by Politico and Morning Consult}}}\uc2 . But she doesn\u8217\'20\'19t fare well among independent voters writ large: 62% think Harris is unlikely to win a presidential election, 51% think she would make a bad president, and 34% think she should be removed as vice-president.\par \par \uc2 As is so often the case, it might end up coming down to whether independent women are motivated to vote at all.\par \par \uc2 Self-identified, true independent voters \u8211\'20\'13 those who don\u8217\'20\'19t lean in one partisan direction \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2021/06/30/behind-bidens-2020-victory/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 helped shift the 2020 election}}}\uc2  in Biden\u8217\'20\'19s favor, with 52% voting for Biden and 43% for Trump, after being evenly divided between Trump and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in 2016. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/election/2020/exit-polls/president/national-results/21"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Exit polls indicated}}}\uc2  that, in 2020, 60% of female independent voters backed Biden.\par \par \uc2 But this year, independent women don\u8217\'20\'19t seem driven to head to the polls. Compared with previous presidential elections, 31% of true independent women are \u8220\'20\'1Cless motivated\u8221\'20\'1D to vote, more than Republicans or Democrats, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/2024-survey-of-women-voters-dashboard/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to recent polling from KFF}}}\uc2  (the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 ). Nearly 40% of independent women say they are \u8220\'20\'1Cuninterested\u8221\'20\'1D in the elections. Just 21% of Democrats and 14% of Republicans feel the same way.\par \par \uc2 Since roughly a dozen states may have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ballot measures this year, including battleground states like Nevada and Arizona, Democrats hope that excitement around those measures \u8211\'20\'13 which have previously succeeded even in deep-red states \u8211\'20\'13 will boost voter turnout and translate to more support for Biden and Harris. Replacing Biden with a woman, like Harris or Michigan governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , could be especially helpful, said Marc Hetherington, a political science professor at the \uc2 University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill\uc2 . (Biden has insisted that he will stay in the race.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe messenger about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the importance of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and things along those lines \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s just a much more powerful message coming from a gen X-aged woman than an 81-year-old man,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be Biden and Harris\u8217\'20\'19s silver bullet.\par \par \uc2 One in 10 female voters now say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is their top issue, KFF found, ahead of issues like gun policy and the war in Gaza. But polling from KFF, the Public Religion Research Institute (PRRI) and the firm PerryUndem all consistently found that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is less important to independent women than to their Democratic peers. Instead, KFF and PerryUndem polling indicates that the economy \u8211\'20\'13 already the top issue among most voters \u8211\'20\'13 is far more important to this group than any other issue.\par \par \uc2 Gamarra, in Florida, believes that his state will pass a measure to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the state constitution. But he said that measure\u8217\'20\'19s success won\u8217\'20\'19t lead to increased support for Democrats among voters in Florida or in the other states set to have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ballot measures.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re going to cross party lines to vote for the amendment, but then they\u8217\'20\'19re going to remain as Republicans or as independents and they won\u8217\'20\'19t vote for the Democratic candidates,\u8221\'20\'1D Gamarra said. \u8220\'20\'1CEspecially, I would add, if the Democrats don\u8217\'20\'19t solve their leadership issue very quickly.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240707ek77000rw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
