\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Arizona proposal to protect  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights in state constitution advances\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 586 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 July 2024\par 14:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 27\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Coalition behind measure says it turned in more than double the needed signatures to get it on November ballot\par \par \uc2 A proposal to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the state constitution of Arizona, a key battleground state in the upcoming US elections, has inched closer to becoming an official ballot measure.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Arizona for \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access, the coalition behind the measure, announced that it had turned in more than 800,000 signatures \u8211\'20\'13 more than double the amount needed to get the measure on the ballot come November.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s more signatures than have ever been submitted for a citizen-led ballot measure in Arizona, according to Chris Love, a spokesperson for Arizona for \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt represents one in five Arizona voters,\u8221\'20\'1D Love said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s an amazing feat for us. I think it\u8217\'20\'19s a demonstration of the strength of our campaign and the excitement of Arizona voters to really settle the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights on the ballot in November.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Arizona currently bans most abortions past 15 weeks of pregnancy, but the state came close to outlawing almost all abortions earlier this spring. In April, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/09/arizona-supreme-court-abortion-decision"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arizona supreme court ruled to uphold a law}}}\uc2  that paved the way for a 1864 near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban \u8211\'20\'13 passed before Arizona even became a state \u8211\'20\'13 to take effect. That controversial decision kicked off a weeks-long battle in the Arizona state legislature, where Republicans hold a one-seat majority in both the state house and senate, as Democratic lawmakers tried to pass a repeal of the 1864 ban. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/02/arizona-1864-abortion-ban-repealed-governor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 They ultimately succeeded}}}\uc2  after a handful of Republican legislators broke ranks and voted for the repeal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur message has always been the same: pregnant patients deserve the freedom to make their individual and personal healthcare decisions, and especially decisions about abortions, with their families and their healthcare providers,\u8221\'20\'1D Love said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe back-and-forth that just happened with respect to the 1864 ban is a clear demonstration of why we need politicians out of the calculus.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 If voters pass the ballot measure, which is officially titled the Arizona \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Act, it would eliminate the state\u8217\'20\'19s 15-week ban and instead protect the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability, a benchmark that typically occurs at about 24 weeks of pregnancy. It would also allow abortions to take place after fetal viability if a healthcare professional believes the procedure is necessary to protect a pregnant person\u8217\'20\'19s life or physical or mental health.\par \par \uc2 Roughly a dozen states, including Arizona and fellow swing state Nevada, are expected to hold ballot measures over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the November elections. Activists in Nebraska and Arkansas are also set to turn in signatures supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ballot measures this week.\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade two years ago, several states \u8211\'20\'13 including traditional Republican strongholds such as Kansas, Kentucky and Ohio \u8211\'20\'13 have successfully passed ballot measures to preserve or strengthen \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Democrats are now hoping that enthusiasm for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will boost voter turnout and translate to support for their own candidates, particularly as Joe Biden continues to trail \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the polls and has faced calls to step down after a devastating debate performance last week.\par \par \uc2 Arizona county election officials now have until 22 August to officially verify the signatures. Part of the reason for turning in so many signatures, Love said, was to counter any efforts to legally challenge the signatures\u8217\'20\'19 legitimacy.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240703ek73002h9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
