\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  draft opinion would allow emergency abortions in Idaho \u8211\'20\'13 report\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman and Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,020 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2024\par 22:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Copy of opinion reinstating lower court\u8217\'20\'19s order posted briefly on supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s website\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  is reportedly set to rule to permit abortions in medical emergencies in Idaho, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-06-26/us-supreme-court-poised-to-allow-emergency-abortions-in-idaho?embedded-checkout=truehttps://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-06-26/us-supreme-court-poised-to-allow-emergency-abortions-in-idaho?embedded-checkout=true"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to a report from Bloomberg}}}\uc2 , citing a copy of the opinion that was posted briefly on the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s website on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 A majority of justices will reportedly dismiss the case as \u8220\'20\'1Cimprovidently granted\u8221\'20\'1D, meaning the supreme court should not have accepted the case. This dismissal would reinstate a lower court\u8217\'20\'19s order that had allowed Idaho hospitals to perform abortions in cases where a woman\u8217\'20\'19s health may be endangered. The state\u8217\'20\'19s law currently only allows abortions when a woman\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Clife\u8221\'20\'1D is in danger \u8211\'20\'13 a much higher threshold.\par \par \uc2 A dismissal would allow litigation to continue in lower courts , which means it could theoretically reach the high court again at a later date.\par \par \uc2 The copy of the opinion obtained by Bloomberg may not be final and could be changed. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://assets.bwbx.io/documents/users/iqjWHBFdfxIU/rJo5436tVr08/v0/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the copy posted online}}}\uc2  by Bloomberg, the justices voted 6-3 to dismiss the case. Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  wrote one opinion in favor of dismissal, joined in full by Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and in part by Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jackson expressed reservations with the decision to dismiss the case \u8211\'20\'13 even though she joined it \u8211\'20\'13 since it leaves the door open for the issue to come back before the court.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s decision is not a victory for pregnant patients in Idaho. It is delay,\u8221\'20\'1D Jackson wrote in an opinion of her own, according to the copy posted by Bloomberg. \u8220\'20\'1CWhile this court dawdles and the country waits, pregnant people experiencing emergency medical conditions remain in a precarious position, as their doctors are kept in the dark about what the law requires.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Beth Brinkmann, senior litigation director for US programs at the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 , agreed with Brown.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have yet to see a final ruling from the court, but if this were to be the final ruling, we should not celebrate it as a win,\u8221\'20\'1D Brinkman said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe language we saw today does not affirm that hospitals are required to treat pregnant people who need emergency abortions. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t say anything either way and punts the issue down the road. The same question is at issue in a case out of Texas, which may be at the supreme court as early as next term.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  also wrote an opinion in favor of dismissal, which Justices \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  joined. In Barrett\u8217\'20\'19s view, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2024/apr/24/us-supreme-court-abortion-rights-emtala"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the oral arguments in the case}}}\uc2  helped change the justices\u8217\'20\'19 understanding of the case enough that she wants a lower court to weigh in before the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe opinion in Moyle v United States, No 23-726, and Idaho v United States, No 23-727, has not been released,\u8221\'20\'1D Patricia McCabe, a court spokesperson, said in a statement to the Guardian, referring to the title of the Idaho \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case. \u8220\'20\'1CThe court\u8217\'20\'19s publications unit inadvertently and briefly uploaded a document to the court\u8217\'20\'19s website. The court\u8217\'20\'19s opinion in these cases will be issued in due course.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This appears to be the second such blunder from the court, once a fortress of secrecy, in as many years. A draft of the supreme court decision overturning Roe v Wade infamously leaked to Politico in May 2022, about a month before the final decision was issued. The final version of the opinion was virtually unchanged from the leaked copy.\par \par \uc2 Idaho has sought to have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  exempted from the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act (Emtala), which requires hospitals that receive federal dollars to stabilize the health of patients who show up at their emergency rooms with medical emergencies. The precedent sought by Idaho, critics said, would endanger pregnant people in any state that has \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. The Biden administration sued Idaho to enforce the law.\par \par \uc2 Emtala is effectively Americans\u8217\'20\'19 only universal right to healthcare. It {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/jan/09/emergency-abortion-supreme-court-case-emtala-idaho"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 came into}}}\uc2  the crosshairs soon after the supreme court overturned {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe}}}\uc2 . For nearly 50 years, the Roe ruling had provided a federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to the point that a fetus can survive outside the womb, which tends to occur around 24 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The Emtala law, signed by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponent Ronald Reagan, sought to protect pregnant women in active labor in particular. Until its passage, hospitals often transferred or \u8220\'20\'1Cdumped\u8221\'20\'1D women who could not pay when they suffered an emergency on public hospitals, even when in advanced stages of labor.\par \par \uc2 Emtala has endured a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://immigrationforum.org/article/fact-sheet-undocumented-immigrants-and-federal-health-care-benefits/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 series of attacks}}}\uc2 , including by some hospital administrators who viewed it as an \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://westjem.com/editorial/why-the-emtala-mandate-for-emergency-care-does-not-equal-healthcare-coverage.html#:~:text=EMTALA%20was%20passed%20in%201986,be%20an%20%E2%80%9Cunfunded%20mandate.%E2%80%9D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unfunded mandate}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. Although the federal government required hospitals to treat sick patients, it never provided money to care for indigent patients.\par \par \uc2 Justices \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  all opposed dismissing the Emtala case, according to a copy of the draft decision posted by Bloomberg. In a fiery dissent, Alito argued that the text of Emtala, as well as the circumstances surrounding its enactment in 1986, makes clear that \u8220\'20\'1Cit does not require hospitals to perform abortions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The majority\u8217\'20\'19s move to dismiss the case, Alito wrote, is \u8220\'20\'1Cbaffling\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CApparently, the court has simply lost the will to decide the easy but emotional and highly politicized question that the case presents,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote Alito, who wrote the majority opinion overturning Roe. \u8220\'20\'1CThat is regrettable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 If the supreme court does move to dismiss the Emtala case, it would be the second defeat for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents this year, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jun/13/supreme-court-abortion-pill-access"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the justices unanimously ruled earlier}}}\uc2  this month to uphold access to a popular \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill. However, that the justices appear to have rejected both cases on technical grounds, rather than on their merits, could leave the door option to similar cases brought by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists in the future.\par \par \uc2 The nation\u8217\'20\'19s highest court, which typically issues all of its term opinions by the end of June, still has 11 more opinions to formally release. Decisions are expected Thursday and Friday.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240626ek6q002e9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
