\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Over half of US women on probation or parole need permission to travel for  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 study\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 565 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2024\par 18:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Policies are \u8216\'20\'18one-two punch\u8217\'20\'19 for women caught in criminal justice system, report\u8217\'20\'19s author says\par \par \uc2 The number of women on probation or parole who must seek permission to travel for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  more than doubled to 635,000 in two years since the supreme court overturned the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prisonpolicy.org/blog/2024/06/18/dobbs/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new report}}}\uc2  finds.\par \par \uc2 Fourteen states have near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and 21 restrict the procedure. Together with near ubiquitous travel restrictions imposed by probation and parole, more than half of women on probation or parole in the US must seek permission to travel before obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The policies are a \u8220\'20\'1Cone-two punch\u8221\'20\'1D for women caught in the criminal justice system, the report\u8217\'20\'19s author said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDoing the math, I was shocked it was so many,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wendy Sawyer, research director at the Prison Policy Initiative, which published the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prisonpolicy.org/blog/2024/06/18/dobbs/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new report}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The research came just ahead of today \u8211\'20\'13 the second anniversary of the Dobbs decision, which the supreme court used to overturn Roe v Wade in 2022.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAn additional 400,000 women is a lot of additional women to be put in this very precarious situation,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Travel has become one of the key ways Americans have continued to legally terminate pregnancies, even amid \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. An {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2024/06/13/us/abortion-state-laws-ban-travel.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated 171,000 Americans}}}\uc2  traveled for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2023 \u8211\'20\'13 many from southern states with sprawling criminal justice systems.\par \par \uc2 Before Roe v Wade was overturned, it was already difficult for many women on probation and parole to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates successfully closed clinics and limited access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly in the south and midwest, even before the constitutional right was overturned.\par \par \uc2 Now, hundreds of thousands more women face the additional burden of getting permission to travel for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a corrections officer. An estimated 82% of women on probation and 85% of women on parole live in states that ban or restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and also restrict travel for people under supervision.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe know that a lot of the states that have enacted some of the strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions are also the most punitive,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sawyer. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re talking about a lot of states in the south where we have total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans or six-week bans, but they also are states that put a lot of people \u8211\'20\'13 not only behind bars \u8211\'20\'13 but under community supervision.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Across the country, the proportion of women traveling to other states for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care has nearly doubled in recent years \u8211\'20\'13 from one in 10 in 2020 to nearly one in five in the first half of 2023, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/2023/12/high-toll-us-abortion-bans-nearly-one-five-patients-now-traveling-out-state-abortion-care"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guttmacher Institute}}}\uc2 , a reproductive rights research organization.\par \par \uc2 Women under supervision are likely to be poor, the majority with incomes below $20,000 a year, and the primary caregivers of children or other family members. Many struggle with addiction, and are in fact on probation or parole for drug charges, Sawyer said. Women on probation and parole are also more likely to report chronic health conditions and disabilities than the general public.\par \par \uc2 Overall, it is a group of women, Sawyer said, \u8220\'20\'1Cjuggling a lot of responsibilities with not a lot of resources\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 24 July 2024 to correct the statistic on the number of women in the US who travel for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240624ek6o001mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
