\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Texas  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban linked to increase in infant deaths, new study finds\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 907 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2024\par 15:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Study analyzed effects of state\u8217\'20\'19s six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, and may be an indicator of how post-Roe bans affect the US\par \par \uc2 A six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in Texas was linked to a 13% increase in the number of infants who died in their first year of life, a new study published Monday in JAMA Pediatrics suggests.\par \par \uc2 The study, published {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/jun/24/abortion-training-doctors-ban-roe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two years to the day}}}\uc2  since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2023/nov/10/state-abortion-laws-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 permitted more than a dozen states}}}\uc2  across the country to outlaw almost all abortions, is one of the first glimpses into how strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans impact babies\u8217\'20\'19 health.\par \par \uc2 The study also estimated that the ban may have led the number of infants in Texas who died within their first month of life to rise more than 10%.\par \par \uc2 Because Texas enacted its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/01/texas-abortion-law-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 six-week \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  in September 2021, months before Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, scholars have studied what has happened in Texas for clues about how post-Roe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are now affecting the rest of the nation. Some of the researchers involved in the Monday study have previously concluded that the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/article-abstract/2806878"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas ban also led to 10,000 additional births}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTexas is the harbinger of potentially what\u8217\'20\'19s to come with Dobbs,\u8221\'20\'1D said Alison Gemmill, the lead author of the study and an assistant professor at the \uc2 Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health\uc2  said, referring to Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, the case that overturned Roe. \u8220\'20\'1COne of the things we\u8217\'20\'19re doing now, as you can imagine, is looking at Dobbs and infant mortality as a next step.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The study found a 23% jump in infant deaths due to congenital anomalies \u8211\'20\'13 the kind of conditions that are often identified in utero and lead to abortions in states where the procedure is legal, since they can be incompatible with life. But that choice is no longer available to pregnant Texans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPrior to this policy, if an anomaly was detected, people would have had the ability to legally terminate at least up to maybe about 20 weeks, or even maybe 22 weeks gestation,\u8221\'20\'1D said Alison Gemmill, the lead author of the study and an assistant professor at the \uc2 Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CAny infant death is tragic, but then layering on top of that, this pregnant person\u8217\'20\'19s situation where they know that they\u8217\'20\'19re carrying a fetus that is incompatible with life, whereas before, they maybe would have had the option to terminate.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In order to isolate the impact of the Texas ban and establish a causal link between the law and infant mortality, Gemmill and the other researchers analyzed death certificates in Texas and 28 other states from January 2018 to February 2022. They built a \u8220\'20\'1Csynthetic\u8221\'20\'1D statistical model of Texas that calculated the number of infant deaths that may have occurred if Texas had never enacted its six-week ban, then compared that model to the number of deaths that actually occurred in the state.\par \par \uc2 Two hundred and sixteen more infant deaths occurred due to the Texas six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, the researchers estimated.\par \par \uc2 Gemmill plans to investigate how Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise may have led to increases in births among children who did not die but may need continued and substantial medical help to survive.\par \par \uc2 After Roe collapsed, enabling Texas to outlaw almost all abortions, Texas has also become the epicenter of the debate over doctors\u8217\'20\'19 ability to provide medically necessary abortions. Like every other state with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, Texas technically permits abortions in emergencies \u8211\'20\'13 but doctors in Texas and across the country have said that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are worded in such a way that they become unusable. Instead of acting, doctors say they have been forced to wait until patients get sick enough that they can intervene.\par \par \uc2 Last year, dozens of women who said they had been denied medically necessary abortions sued Texas in the hopes of clarifying its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. At least one of the women, denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and unable to leave the state, ended up giving birth to an infant who died within hours of her birth.\par \par \uc2 In May, the Texas supreme court \u8211\'20\'13 which is composed entirely of Republicans \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/31/texas-abortion-ban-medical-exceptions-supreme-court-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rejected their challenge}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After months of delay and calls for action from both \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, the Texas Medical Board on Friday issued guidance to clarify that \u8220\'20\'1Clack of imminent risk of death or substantial impairment to a patient should not preclude a physician from doing what is medically necessary\u8221\'20\'1D, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tmb.state.tx.us/dl/57121A93-FA13-5E39-F2B2-DFC51BCA2EFD"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the board president said in a statement}}}\uc2 . However, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2024/06/21/texas-medical-board-abortion-guidance/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the guidance does not preclude}}}\uc2  Texas prosecutors from pursuing charges against doctors.\par \par \uc2 The researchers behind the Monday study did not have access to records that broke down infant deaths by demographic information such as race or ethnicity, so it is not clear if specific groups were more likely to give birth to babies who later died. However, researchers noted, the infant death rate among non-Hispanic Black women is triple that among non-Hispanic white women. Low-income women are also more likely to have lacked the resources needed to flee Texas for abortions in other states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis research shows that when attempting to legislate something as complex as pregnancy,\u8221\'20\'1D Gemmill said, \u8220\'20\'1Cit is crucial to design policies based on a thorough understanding of public health impacts and robust scientific evidence.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240624ek6o0020k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
