\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 US doctors struggle to get basic  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  training two years after fall of Roe\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,577 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2024\par 12:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Post-Roe, doctors are scrambling to get trained amid bans, closed clinics and overburdened facilities\par \par \uc2 The fall of Roe v Wade upended Dr Jasmine Chan\u8217\'20\'19s career.\par \par \uc2 As a medical student, the Texas native wanted to provide abortions as an OB-GYN. But in 2022, as Chan prepared to apply for residency \u8211\'20\'13 a kind of years-long apprenticeship after medical school \u8211\'20\'13 she worried that the quirks of the residency placement process would keep her in Texas, which banned abortions after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI met with my advisers and I had very strong heart-to-heart conversations about how I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t see myself practicing medicine if it didn\u8217\'20\'19t include me getting to do abortions,\u8221\'20\'1D Chan said. Instead of becoming an OB-GYN, she decided to become a family medicine doctor \u8211\'20\'13 a less competitive specialty that increased her chances of finding a residency in a state that protects \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Now, two years into her residency program in New Jersey, Chan is preparing to finally get the training that she\u8217\'20\'19s longed for her entire career. But, like many other doctors seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training after Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, she will still have to overcome hurdles that didn\u8217\'20\'19t exist just two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Over the next few months, Chan will pursue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training in two different clinics, the closest of which is three hours away, on top of her day job. In the fall, she\u8217\'20\'19ll need to relocate entirely for training at a third clinic.\par \par \uc2 She\u8217\'20\'19ll have to jump through all of those hoops because there just aren\u8217\'20\'19t enough clinics left to train the US\u8217\'20\'19s future doctors in a relatively quick, straightforward procedure that one in four women will undergo at some point in their lives.\par \par \uc2 Clinical training sites are overburdened just trying to meet patient need\par \par \uc2 Latona Giwa of the Midwest Access Project\par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe exactly two years ago, it paved the way for more than a dozen states to outlaw almost all abortions. It also plunged the American medical training system into chaos. Because many hospitals have long refused to perform most abortions, residents who want to learn the procedure have often received their training at \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics. But now that bans have shuttered so many clinics, there are just not enough facilities to train all the residents.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CClinical training sites are overburdened just trying to meet patient need, which makes it far less likely that they will accept as many trainees or for as many days as they used to,\u8221\'20\'1D said Latona Giwa, executive director of the Midwest Access Project, a non-profit that helps a range of healthcare professionals receive reproductive healthcare training, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  instruction.\par \par \uc2 But if they don\u8217\'20\'19t, she said, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe will not have a next generation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Doctors across the country already say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have forced them to delay care for pregnant women in medical emergencies. But as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training becomes more scarce, or accessible only to doctors such as Chan who have time and money to clear a logistical obstacle course, fewer doctors will have the skills to handle dangerous pregnancy complications, including miscarriages, which are often treated with the same procedures used in abortions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training may soon become even harder to access. Project 2025, a playbook written by the influential thinktank \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2  in anticipation of a second Donald Trump administration, recommends that the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Censure that training for medical professionals (doctors, nurses, etc) and doulas is not being used for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 also recommends penalizing institutions that don\u8217\'20\'19t make it easier for medical professionals to opt out of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training on conscience grounds.\par \par \uc2 Although the language is ambiguous, enforcing it could ultimately lead to the prohibition or even criminalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training, said Mary Ziegler, a professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , who studies the legal history of reproduction.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt sounds like, ideally, they want to defund or essentially have like a Hyde amendment for any kind of federal support, whether direct or indirect, for training,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ziegler, referring to a nationwide rule that bans the use of federal dollars for abortions. Residency programs and medical schools, like virtually all US educational institutions, take money from the federal government.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It is just so siloed\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 It has never been exactly easy for American doctors to learn how to perform abortions. The hospital systems that run medical schools and residency programs are deeply hierarchical institutions that rely on public dollars \u8211\'20\'13 and so they tend to steer clear of issues as controversial as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In 1992, two decades after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  first decided Roe, only 12% of OB-GYN residency programs routinely offered training in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Three years later, the \uc2 Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education\uc2  decided, for the first time, to mandate that OB-GYN residency programs teach doctors how to perform abortions.\par \par \uc2 Still, as of 2018, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ajog.org/article/S0002-9378(18)30292-8/fulltext"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 just 64% OB-GYN residencies offered}}}\uc2  routine, dedicated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training. Meanwhile, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1363/psrh.12215"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hospitals provided just 3% of all abortions}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 and, usually, only in high-risk pregnancies or cases of fetal anomalies. That left the burden of training to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.\par \par \uc2 To help close the gap, a national initiative called the Ryan Residency Training Program (RRTP) helps place OB-GYN residents with clinics for training.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, when Texas enacted a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, the RRTP stepped up to help residents travel out of state for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training. But the process turned out to be a bureaucratic nightmare, as programs and clinics struggled to secure the right paperwork and legal clearances.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COh my God, it took six months before even the first resident could travel,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Jody Steinauer, who leads the RRTP. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s how hard it is to set these up.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After Roe fell, the \uc2 Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education\uc2  announced that it would mandate OB-GYN programs to either offer residents training in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or send them to states where they can get it. However, a spokesperson for the council declined to say how many OB-GYN programs currently met that requirement. There are now nearly 1,300 OB-GYN residents living and working in states with near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans \u8211\'20\'13 all of whom must travel out of state for hands-on training in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The RRTP has helped 16 programs in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans set up partnerships to help residents travel out of state for training.\par \par \uc2 States with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, which tend to have among the highest rates of maternal mortality and morbidity in the nation, are now facing a worsening OB-GYN shortage. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aamcresearchinstitute.org/our-work/data-snapshot/post-dobbs-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fewer OB-GYNs are even applying}}}\uc2  to residency programs in those states. Louisiana, for example, saw a 17% drop; Alabama saw a 21% drop; Missouri, 25%.\par \par \uc2 Alongside OB-GYNs, family medicine doctors are the specialists most likely to learn how to perform abortions. But they aren\u8217\'20\'19t required to complete that training \u8211\'20\'13 and the ones who want it, even in blue states, can\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily acquire it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s just a lot more competition for training spots,\u8221\'20\'1D said Erica Chong, executive director of the Reproductive Health Education in Family Medicine (Rhedi) program, the family medicine version of the RRTP.\par \par \uc2 Dr Amy Hoffman is a family medicine resident in Pennsylvania, a state that permits abortions. But although her institution supports her desire to learn to provide abortions, it does not have any faculty with the skills to teach her. At this point in her residency, she\u8217\'20\'19s doubtful that she\u8217\'20\'19ll get the training.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have to find a month where I can leave my residency and give myself months and months ahead of planning to get the medical agreements in place,\u8221\'20\'1D Hoffman said.\par \par \uc2 Her day job is already arduous enough. Residency programs are permitted to require residents to work up to 80 hours a week; they can also make them work 28-hour shifts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery doctor in every specialty has seen a caesarean or seen an appendectomy or taken care of diabetes, but the majority of doctors have never seen \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and never seen the procedure performed,\u8221\'20\'1D Hoffman said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is just so siloed and inaccessible to patients and learners alike.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A jump in interest\par \par \uc2 Advocates across the medical training field are thrilled that, since Roe fell, so many new providers want to get trained in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A handful of new residency programs have already joined the Rhedi program. Steinauer said that there is now \u8220\'20\'1Ca lot more collaboration, within states, within regions, nationally\u8221\'20\'1D between institutions looking to bulk up their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training.\par \par \uc2 The Midwest Access Project received nearly 50 applications in its first application cycle after Roe\u8217\'20\'19s downfall. At the time, that was the most it had ever received.\par \par \uc2 Now the non-profit receives somewhere between 100 and 200 applications each cycle. It\u8217\'20\'19s still only able to accept about 50 people each cycle, but it gives Giwa hope.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s a huge number of great impact, because every time one \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -trained provider is added to a community or region, a state, they can train tenfold providers and they can do it in a primary care setting, ideally,\u8221\'20\'1D Giwa said. She envisions a future in which \u8220\'20\'1Cyou can just go get your allergy medicine and your \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills from the same person, and it\u8217\'20\'19s not this huge extra ordeal\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240624ek6o001gx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
