\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Democrats move to repeal 1873 law they say could pave way for national  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 758 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 June 2024\par 19:49 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Comstock Act, largely unenforced in recent history, could be used to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs in post-Roe US\par \par \uc2 Democrats introduced legislation on Thursday to repeal a 19th-century anti-obscenity law that bans mailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials, amid growing worries that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists will use the law to implement a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 The bill to repeal the Comstock Act was introduced by the Minnesota Democratic senator Tina Smith, whose office provided a draft copy of the legislation to the Guardian. The Massachusetts senator Elizabeth Warren and Nevada senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  also back the bill, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2024/06/20/comstock-abortion-repeal-tina-smith-senate/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the Washington Post}}}\uc2 , which first reported the news of Smith\u8217\'20\'19s plans. Companion legislation was also set to be introduced in the House.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to see that these anti-choice extremists are intending to misapply the Comstock Act,\u8221\'20\'1D Smith said in an interview. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd so our job is to draw attention to that, and to do everything that we can to stop them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Passed in 1873, the Comstock Act is named after the anti-vice crusader Anthony Comstock and, in its original iteration, broadly banned people from using the mail to send anything \u8220\'20\'1Cobscene, lewd or lascivious\u8221\'20\'1D, including \u8220\'20\'1Cany article or thing designed or intended for the prevention of conception or procuring an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. In the 151 years since its enactment, legal rulings and congressional action narrowed the scope of the Comstock Act. For years, legal experts regarded it as a dead letter, especially when Roe v Wade established the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe in 2022, some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists started arguing that the Comstock Act\u8217\'20\'19s prohibition against mailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials remained good law. Project 2025, a playbook written by the influential thinktank \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 , recommends that a future conservative presidential administration use the Comstock Act to block the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. Other activists have gone even further, arguing that the Comstock Act can outlaw the mailing of all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials.\par \par \uc2 Because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics rely on the mail for the drugs and tools they need to do their work, such an interpretation of the Comstock Act would be a de facto ban on all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has issued guidance arguing that someone only violates the Comstock Act if the sender intends for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials \u8220\'20\'1Cto be used unlawfully\u8221\'20\'1D. However, although Joe Biden has focused his re-election campaign on reproductive rights, he has steered clear of addressing the potential return of the Comstock Act.\par \par \uc2 Smith said that it \u8220\'20\'1Cseems impossible\u8221\'20\'1D that her repeal bill will garner the 60 votes necessary to advance legislation in the Senate. Republicans recently stymied Democratic efforts to establish {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jun/05/legal-right-contraception-senate-vote"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 federal rights to contraception}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jun/13/right-to-ivf-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in vitro fertilization}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But Smith views her bill as a chance to raise awareness of the nationwide consequences of a Comstock Act revival, particularly among voters living in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are currently protected.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou talk to somebody in Minnesota or Nevada or Pennsylvania, places where people feel secure that they have control over their own decisions and their own potential to decide for themselves about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 and then come to find out that Donald Trump has a plan to take away that control that you have, even without a vote or an act of Congress,\u8221\'20\'1D Smith said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt makes it much more real, what the difference is and what the contrast is, what the choices are for you even in those states where state law protects you. That could all change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/04/02/opinion/comstock-act-abortion-repeal.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times April op-ed}}}\uc2  where she first aired her plans to repeal the Comstock Act, Smith suggested that she planned to introduce the legislation once the supreme court ruled on a case involving access to mifepristone, one of the two drugs typically used in US medication abortions and a top target of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists. In a unanimous opinion earlier this month, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jun/13/supreme-court-abortion-pill-access"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the supreme court ruled on technical grounds}}}\uc2  to let access to mifepristone remain {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/jun/13/supreme-court-abortion-pill-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unchanged for now}}}\uc2 . Although rightwing justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Samuel Alito{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/26/us-supreme-court-abortion-pill-case-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brought up the Comstock Act}}}\uc2  during oral arguments in the case, neither the majority opinion nor a concurrence by Thomas ultimately mentioned the anti-obscenity law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe court, in its decision, left the door wide open for future challenges based on Comstock,\u8221\'20\'1D Smith said, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CThere was nothing in the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision that gave me any sense of security.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240620ek6k002bk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
