\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Terrified of losing their rights\u8217\'20\'19:  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  is a leading issue for Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s Latino voters\b0\par\par\uc2 Maanvi Singh in Phoenix \par \uc2 1,339 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 June 2024\par 12:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Organizers hope those disillusioned by electoral politics will vote with an eye on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights after fall of Roe\par \par \uc2 Throughout her political career, Raquel Ter\u225\'00\'E1n has been advised to avoid talking too much about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve been told: \u8216\'20\'18Be careful speaking about the issue when you\u8217\'20\'19re running in a Latino district,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D said Ter\u225\'00\'E1n, a congressional candidate in Arizona and former chair of the state\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic party.\par \par \uc2 The conventional political wisdom was that Latino voters \u8211\'20\'13 many of whom are religious \u8211\'20\'13 opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. That has never squared with Ter\u225\'00\'E1n\u8217\'20\'19s experience. \u8220\'20\'1CI go to mass and I believe in a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 But during this election cycle \u8211\'20\'13 perhaps more than ever before \u8211\'20\'13 the issue could be a decisive factor for Latino voters in Arizona. Longtime political operatives, progressive Latino organizers and Latino candidates like Ter\u225\'00\'E1n say they\u8217\'20\'19ve seen it compel even those who have grown disillusioned by electoral politics.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become a top priority for a lot of the people who vote all the time, and also the people who don\u8217\'20\'19t vote all the time,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ter\u225\'00\'E1n. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause voters are terrified of losing their rights.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This summer, the state supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s reinstatement of an extreme, civil war-era \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law has fueled support for a November ballot initiative that could enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Though lawmakers repealed the law in May, it will remain in effect though the end of June before it is replaced by a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have had people running out of the house to sign the petition,\u8221\'20\'1D said Alejandra Gomez, the executive director of the progressive group Lucha, which has been collecting endorsements for the ballot initiative. \u8220\'20\'1CThat has never happened to me before.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even though the campaign collected enough signatures in April for the measure to appear on the ballot in November, organizers said they would continue to gather support \u8211\'20\'13 anticipating legal challenges and audits from Republicans seeking to disqualify the initiative.\par \par \uc2 On a searing afternoon in late May, as the temperatures in the metro Phoenix area began to tip into the triple digits, thousands of volunteers deployed to coffee shops, libraries and local breweries to gather more signatures. Canvassers for Democratic candidates carried \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access for All petitions alongside their normal literature.\par \par \uc2 Enthusiasm for the initiative has surprised even longtime political operatives in the state. Views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  within Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s Latino communities can vary widely, especially across generational lines, said Enrique Davis Mazlum, the Arizona state director for \uc2 UnidosUS\uc2 , a Hispanic civil rights and advocacy group.\par \par \uc2 What many polls have historically failed to capture is that even if voters don\u8217\'20\'19t support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  personally, they still believe in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. \u8220\'20\'1CEven if they are Catholic and might not themselves choose to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they don\u8217\'20\'19t want the government to decide for them,\u8221\'20\'1D Davis Mazlum said.\par \par \uc2 Polls now indicate that growing majorities of Latino voters support the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Surveys from the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  have found that support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access among Hispanic voters nationally has ticked up over the past two decades \u8211\'20\'13 from about 47% in 2007-2008 to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/2022/09/29/hispanics-views-on-key-issues-facing-the-nation/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about 60%}}}\uc2  in 2022, after the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, a 2022 poll by \uc2 UnidosUS\uc2  and Mi Familia Vota {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://unidosus.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/unidosus_national_surveyoflatinovoters.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  that 80% of Latino voters in Arizona agreed or somewhat agreed that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should remain legal, regardless of their personal beliefs on the issue.\par \par \uc2 That poll, which was released shortly after the fall of Roe v Wade, captured a moment when voters were especially worried about reproductive rights. The reinstatement of the 1864 near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban earlier this year has been another inflection point. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve seen that issue again at the top of mind of a lot of voters and particularly Latino voters,\u8221\'20\'1D said Stella Rouse, director of the Hispanic Research Center at \uc2 Arizona State University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Junelle Cavero Harnal, a Democrat from south Phoenix who was appointed to a vacated seat in Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s House of Representatives just one day before the legislature voted on a repeal of the civil war-era \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, said it was an issue that her constituents have raised on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 As she campaigns for a full term in a diverse district where more than 80% of her constituents are people of color and about 58% are Latino, she said: \u8220\'20\'1CUsually what people want to talk to me about is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the cost of living and the conflict in Israel.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That bore true on a searing weekday evening at the end of May as Cavero Harnal \u8211\'20\'13 in a baseball cap and campaign T-shirt \u8211\'20\'13 was knocking on doors in her own neighborhood. \u8220\'20\'1CI have two daughters and a granddaughter,\u8221\'20\'1D one woman volunteered after answering the door. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd all our rights are being taken away.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For Sophia Mendoza, 17 \u8211\'20\'13 whose family has known Cavero Harnal for years \u8211\'20\'13 the reinstatement of the 1864 ban came as a shock. \u8220\'20\'1CI hadn\u8217\'20\'19t even been paying much attention to the news when the ban happened,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mendoza.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI talked about it with my mom and sister and my mom brought up the fact that she\u8217\'20\'19s had multiple miscarriages, and how we shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t take away anyone\u8217\'20\'19s right to have or not have children,\u8221\'20\'1D chimed in Mendoza\u8217\'20\'19s friend Emily Pe\u241\'00\'F1a, 18. \u8220\'20\'1CIt brought up so many feelings\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Organizers said they hope the anger voters feel will drive turnout in November.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen we\u8217\'20\'19re at the doors, and we begin to share that President Biden actually supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, that completely changes the conversation,\u8221\'20\'1D Cavero Harna said. Even voters who are disappointed with the president\u8217\'20\'19s progress on the economy or immigration have signalled they would consider voting for Biden. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the way that our communities are coming around to entertaining voting for the top of the ticket,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s campaign appears to have picked up that the issue could be a factor in its favor, coming out with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign that targets Latino men. Ads running in Arizona and Nevada {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2024/05/03/biden-looks-to-abortion-issue-in-new-ad-to-help-boost-hispanic-support/73548320007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 feature}}}\uc2  Cesar Carreon \u8211\'20\'13 a carpenter who identifies himself as a marine who served in Iraq and Afghanistan. \u8220\'20\'1CI know what tough is,\u8221\'20\'1D Carreon says in the ad. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd a guy like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that attacks women, takes away their freedom and brags about it? That\u8217\'20\'19s not tough.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He continues: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m with Joe Biden, because he\u8217\'20\'19ll give my daughters their freedom back.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It may help Democrats and progressives that voters can also see through Republican efforts to appeal to their community with social conservatism, said Alfredo Gutierrez, a former Democratic state senate leader who has over the decades fought back numerous Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 efforts to erode \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 As the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access ballot measure gains steam, Republicans have promoted a \u8220\'20\'1CSecure the Border\u8221\'20\'1D initiative to galvanize conservatives that would empower law enforcement to profile and arrest anyone they suspect might have crossed the border illegally. They\u8217\'20\'19ve also pushed a number of efforts to roll back voting rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s always amazing to me that the same people who on the one hand are attacking immigrants, attacking us for being here, those same people believe we will support them on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue,\u8221\'20\'1D Gutierrez said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s an aberration that borders on psychosis.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Then again, he said, his community has long supported the right to choose. Back when he was state senate leader, he recalled once squirming in the pews when the priest at his Catholic church spoke on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CHe talked about what a sin it was, and how still some members of the church supported it, and I knew he was talking about me,\u8221\'20\'1D Gutierrez said.\par \par \uc2 After the sermon, as Gutierrez was eating menudo in the church basement, a group of elder women in the congregation pulled him aside, he recalled: \u8220\'20\'1CThey said: \u8216\'20\'18Don\u8217\'20\'19t listen to that. You\u8217\'20\'19re doing the right thing.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240618ek6i001md\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
