\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pill ruling isn\u8217\'20\'19t the end of a fight. It\u8217\'20\'19s the beginning\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,070 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 June 2024\par 16:17 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The justices unanimously rejected the mifepristone case on technical grounds. Their ruling is not the victory it may seem\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the \uc2 US supreme court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jun/13/supreme-court-abortion-pill-access"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unanimously ruled}}}\uc2  that the Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine \u8211\'20\'13 a sock-puppet group of fanatically anti-choice doctors and busybodies \u8211\'20\'13 lacked standing to sue in the group\u8217\'20\'19s challenge to the Food and Drug Administration\u8217\'20\'19s approval of mifepristone, the drug that is now used in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/2024/03/medication-abortion-accounted-63-all-us-abortions-2023-increase-53-2020"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 60%}}}\uc2  of American abortions. The court\u8217\'20\'19s decision may seem like the end of this battle. It\u8217\'20\'19s only the beginning.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit in question had originally emerged from Amarillo, Texas, in a federal court that has become a destination for anti-choice litigants because Matthew Kacsmaryk, a Trump appointee and the sole judge hearing federal civil cases in the district, is a militant anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist. Kacsmaryk made news when he used to case as a pretext to issue a nationwide injunction revoking \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of the drug. The fifth circuit court, a radically rightwing appeals panel which has jurisdiction over Texas and which has repeatedly sought to push the supreme court to new heights of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremism, upheld most of Kacsmaryk\u8217\'20\'19s ruling, but limited the case to challenges that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  made in 2016 and 2021 to make mifepristone more easily available.\par \par \uc2 The case that reached the supreme court was about whether the drug, which has been found to be safer than Tylenol, could be used between seven and 10 weeks of pregnancy, and \u8211\'20\'13 crucially \u8211\'20\'13 whether it could be prescribed by telehealth and sent through the mail.\par \par \uc2 There was only one problem: the plaintiffs had no legal standing whatsoever. Their merits case was bad enough \u8211\'20\'13 they relied on obscure, since-retracted studies to claim that mifepristone was dangerous. But their theory of how they were harmed by the drug\u8217\'20\'19s availability \u8211\'20\'13 even though they neither took nor prescribed it \u8211\'20\'13 was fanciful, heavily contingent and ultimately too far-fetched even for this very enthusiastically anti-choice supreme court. \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , the Trump-appointed justice best known for being {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/sep/26/brett-kavanaugh-latest-accusation-julie-swetnick-sexual-misconduct-michael-avenatti"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 accused of sexual assault}}}\uc2  and braying that he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.buzzfeednews.com/article/skbaer/brett-kavanaugh-likes-beer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 likes beer}}}\uc2 , authored {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/23-235_n7ip.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the opinion}}}\uc2  that maintained the status quo with regard to the drug\u8217\'20\'19s availability. Mifepristone is still banned in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. But it has not been made more difficult to get in states without them.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a reason why anti-choice groups are targeting mifepristone. In the more than two decades since the drug was first approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , it has revolutionized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. Whereas previously, abortions were largely surgical procedures, mifepristone was the first safe and effective method for terminating a pregnancy by ingesting a pill. It allowed abortions to be conducted at a patient\u8217\'20\'19s convenience, and in the privacy of her home.\par \par \uc2 A slim majority of abortions in the US were already conducted with mifepristone before Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, but in the years since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s reversal, the use of the pills has risen even further: now, 63% of abortions are performed using mifepristone. And because of the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 2021 ruling allowing the drug to be administered via telemedicine \u8211\'20\'13 one of the modes of access that the AHM suit was seeking to end \u8211\'20\'13 not all of those mifepristone abortions are happening in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s because, since Dobbs, providers in Democratic-controlled states \u8211\'20\'13 particularly those that have shield laws that protect providers from out-of-state liability \u8211\'20\'13 have been prescribing the pills to women in Republican-controlled states via telemedicine, sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills into those ban states via the mail. Some estimates posit that more than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://societyfp.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/WeCount-report-6-May-2024-Dec-2023-data_Final.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 8,000 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  seekers}}}\uc2  in ban states receive the pills this way every month \u8211\'20\'13 safely and secretly ending their pregnancies in defiance of local law.\par \par \uc2 Because mifepristone is safe and effective \u8211\'20\'13 because it is easy to use, easy to access and difficult to track \u8211\'20\'13 the anti-choice movement will not stop looking for ways to restrict or ban it. And despite the justices\u8217\'20\'19 ruling in this latest case, it is likely that the supreme court will look to help them. This particular case isn\u8217\'20\'19t even dead: back at the district court, Judge Kacsmaryk has already allowed three states \u8211\'20\'13 Missouri, Kansas and Idaho \u8211\'20\'13 to join as plaintiffs, and their claims will go forward. It is likely, too, that the anti-choice lawyers bringing the suit will soon find plaintiffs with more plausible standing claims.\par \par \uc2 When those plaintiffs reach the supreme court, there is every indication that they will find a sympathetic audience for their merits claims in the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative supermajority. As {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/maryrziegler/status/1801265339239633163"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mary Ziegler}}}\uc2 , a law professor and historian of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, put it, \u8220\'20\'1COne could read parts of this opinion as creating a roadmap [for] future plaintiffs who do want to establish standing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 We already know that this court wants to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide, and to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access as much as possible in the meantime. And at least some of the justices will all but openly work in tandem with the conservative legal movement to find the right opportunity to do it.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the choice to remand the case for lack of standing may be meant to give the court a little bit of much-needed good PR. As the nation careens toward the November elections, a contest in which Americans\u8217\'20\'19 anger over Dobbs is expected to play a crucial part, the court is reeling from a series of cascading ethics scandals. Their approval is in the toilet; their justices keep {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jun/13/clarence-thomas-harlan-crow-trips"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 getting caught accepting lavish vacations from billionaires}}}\uc2 , or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/article/2024/may/25/samuel-alito-upside-down-us-flag"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seemingly endorsing coups with cryptic flag displays}}}\uc2  outside their mansions.\par \par \uc2 Later this term, the justices are expected to partially nullify a federal law creating minimum standards of care in emergency rooms, so as to allow states to ban life- and health-saving abortions. That case, over the federal statute called Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act, is likely to cost hundreds or thousands of Americans their lives.\par \par \uc2 In these circumstances, the justices might not mind taking some of the heat off themselves by kicking the can down the road another year or two before they restrict mifepristone. After all, it\u8217\'20\'19s not like they won\u8217\'20\'19t get another chance.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240614ek6e002js\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
