\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Complete  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban in South Carolina more likely after primaries\b0\par\par\uc2 George Chidi \par \uc2 907 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 June 2024\par 07:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Two of five \u8216\'20\'18sister senators\u8217\'20\'19 who blocked legislation to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from conception lost to far-right challengers\par \par \uc2 The right to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in South Carolina is in a \u8220\'20\'1Cdire\u8221\'20\'1D condition, said the state senator Penry Gustafson, who lost her seat on Tuesday to a primary challenger prepared to vote to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at conception.\par \par \uc2 Gustafson is one of five female lawmakers dubbed the \u8220\'20\'1Csister senators\u8221\'20\'1D who blocked legislation to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the point of conception in the state. The three Republicans among them \u8211\'20\'13 Gustafson, Sandy Senn and Katrina Shealy \u8211\'20\'13 each drew male primary challengers who competed for conservative primary voters seeking more restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Senn also lost on Tuesday to a primary challenger, state representative Matt Leber, by 31 votes. Results in that race will go through a mandatory recount.\par \par \uc2 If two or more senators are replaced by supporters of a complete \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, opponents will no longer have enough votes in the senate to block it. South Carolina currently has a six-week \u8220\'20\'1Cheartbeat\u8221\'20\'1D standard, which the group of female senators have said they believe is too restrictive and unpopular even among South Carolina conservatives.\par \par \uc2 Leber questions that argument.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think our current \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws reflect the values of South Carolina and the low country in general,\u8221\'20\'1D Leber said.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina\u8217\'20\'19s ban an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after embryonic cardiac activity can be detected provides exceptions for rape up to 12 weeks of pregnancy, for incest up to 12 weeks of pregnancy, in cases of fatal fetal anomaly and in cases protecting the mother\u8217\'20\'19s life.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court struck down the state\u8217\'20\'19s six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in January 2023, ruling it a violation of the right to privacy under the state constitution despite the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturning Roe v Wade\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections a year earlier. State lawmakers called a special session in May 2023 to draft new legislation.\par \par \uc2 The three Republican \u8220\'20\'1Csister senators\u8221\'20\'1D, none of whom can credibly be described as pro-choice, had each voted for the original \u8220\'20\'1Cheartbeat\u8221\'20\'1D legislation, but argued in the special session for fewer restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and blocked legislation calling for a ban at contraception. The South Carolina state senate requires three-fifths of its 46 members to vote to end debate; three Republican crossovers were enough to filibuster the bill.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether he would vote for a bill barring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at conception, Leber replied: \u8220\'20\'1CWell, I am a Christian. I do believe that life begins at conception. I just don\u8217\'20\'19t think we have that mandate.\u8221\'20\'1D But he said he would not discuss hypothetical legislation.\par \par \uc2 Only six women currently serve among South Carolina\u8217\'20\'19s 46 state senators, among the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cawp.rutgers.edu/facts/state-state-information/south-carolina"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 smallest proportion}}}\uc2  of any state, according to the Center for American Women and Politics.\par \par \uc2 Shealy, who has served in the senate for 12 years, came in first in a three-way race but did not win a majority. She will face Carlisle Kennedy, an assistant solicitor, in a 25 June runoff.\par \par \uc2 Gustafson flipped a traditionally Democratic district four years ago. After legislative redistricting, she lost much of the territory where she was most familiar to voters. She lost a primary challenge 82-18 on Tuesday night to Allen Blackmon, a second-term Lancaster county council member.\par \par \uc2 Her loss represents political regression, she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSouth Carolina is in trouble,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve been set back \u8230\'20\'26 I don\u8217\'20\'19t know how much time, maybe decades \u8230\'20\'26 in our advances with women and our voices representing our state.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Recounts rarely overturn election results. Officials will begin retabulating the roughly 8,000 votes after the election is certified by the state on Friday, officials said.\par \par \uc2 Turnout in the primary statewide was about 11%.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CModerate Republicans didn\u8217\'20\'19t vote,\u8221\'20\'1D Gustafson said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat was particularly disheartening and disruptive.\u8221\'20\'1D Low-turnout primaries create results that reward more politically extreme candidates and more politically extreme messaging.\par \par \uc2 The well-watched race between Senn and Leber, in the suburban middle-class outskirts of fast-growing Charleston, was looked to as a bellwether for conservative appetites on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  question. But it was also a bruising affair.\par \par \uc2 In a Trump-inflected campaign, Leber described Senn as a quasi-Democrat who was insufficiently anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senn argued that Leber was a political extremist with connections to militia groups \u8211\'20\'13 which Leber denied \u8211\'20\'13 and argued erroneously that Leber had faked his college degree. Senn did not return calls and email seeking comment.\par \par \uc2 Gustafson described Leber in stark terms as a purveyor of radical rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMatt Leber is a nutcase. He is scary,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CHe wants women who get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be in prison. He is awful. I don\u8217\'20\'19t know what the hell he\u8217\'20\'19s going to do in our state senate. Our senate is not like that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In response to Gustafson\u8217\'20\'19s comments, Leber said: \u8220\'20\'1CIt is not my position that women should be punished for abortions \u8230\'20\'26 ever.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 National observers have been looking for signs of a liberal backlash to the fall of Roe v Wade protections, as was seen in referendums in Kansas, Ohio and elsewhere erecting new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights protections. But that backlash is not happening within Republican primary circles, at least not in South Carolina, Leber said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere was a backlash,\u8221\'20\'1D Leber said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd Senator Senn lost her seat.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 13 June 2024. The South Carolina state senate has 46 members, not 48.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240613ek6d001e6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
