\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Senate Republicans block bill that establishes right to IVF across the US\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman in New York \par \uc2 632 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 June 2024\par 21:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Right to IVF act was not expected to pass, but Democrats forced vote to get GOP on record opposing treatment\par \par \uc2 Senate Republicans have defeated a bill that would have established a federal right to in vitro fertilization, a piece of legislation that Democrats forced to the floor on Thursday as part of an election-year effort to contrast their approach to reproductive rights with that of the party across the aisle.\par \par \uc2 The bill, the Right to IVF act, would have overwritten any state efforts to restrict the right to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/ivf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 IVF}}}\uc2  as well as seeking to make the treatment more affordable and accessible, including for US military service members and veterans.\par \par \uc2 The legislation was introduced by Senators \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  of Washington state, \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  of New Jersey and Tammy Duckworth of Illinois, and the bill was not expected to pass, given that most legislation needs at least 60 votes to advance in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Instead, Democrats hoped to get Republicans on the record opposing an infertility treatment that is widely popular among Americans. They deployed a similar strategy last week, when {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jun/05/legal-right-contraception-senate-vote"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats held a vote on a bill}}}\uc2  that would have guaranteed a nationwide right to contraception \u8211\'20\'13 which, like IVF, is very popular. That bill, the Right to Contraception act, also failed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLast week, every senator was put on the record as to whether they will defend the right to contraception. And despite Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 words about supporting birth control, their actions \u8211\'20\'13 voting against the Right to Contraception act \u8211\'20\'13 spoke louder,\u8221\'20\'1D Murray said in a speech from the Senate floor on Thursday. \u8220\'20\'1CToday, we are putting Republicans on the record on another issue families across the country are deeply concerned about: the right to IVF.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Louisiana senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 , a Republican, denounced the bill, which he said was motivated by \u8220\'20\'1Cpolitical purposes\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not serious legislation,\u8221\'20\'1D Cassidy said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was not brought through the committee process. It is a political process.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Because IVF typically involves creating embryos that may not be implanted in a woman\u8217\'20\'19s uterus or may go unused after genetic testing, some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners have long opposed IVF. However, the US \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars have rarely focused on IVF.\par \par \uc2 Then, in February, the Alabama state supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/feb/23/fertility-doctors-reaction-alabama-embryo-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled that}}}\uc2  frozen embryos created through IVF are legally \u8220\'20\'1Cextrauterine children\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a decision that endorsed the tenets of so-called fetal personhood, promoted by a US movement that seeks to endow embryos and fetuses with full legal rights and protections. The ruling led many IVF providers in Alabama to temporarily pause their operations, which created chaos and triggered backlash across the country.\par \par \uc2 Still, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have continued to gain ground in their battle on IVF. On Wednesday, the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2 , the largest Protestant group in the US, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jun/12/southern-baptist-convention-ivf-vote"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted at its annual meeting to condemn IVF}}}\uc2 . With its nearly 13 million members and enormous political influence, the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s rejection of IVF signaled a turning point in the debate over IVF. Although evangelical Protestants have largely supported IVF, the vote suggests that the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement is successfully making the case that opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  necessitates opposition to IVF.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not the end of our fight for family building for all. We will continue until everyone in this country has access to the family building options they need and the availability of IVF is guaranteed in all 50 states,\u8221\'20\'1D Barbara Collura, president and CEO of \uc2 Resolve: The National Infertility Association\uc2 , said in a statement Thursday, after the failed Senate vote. \u8220\'20\'1CIntroducing this bill was already a big win for advocates of increasing access to fertility treatments. Our work led to this comprehensive legislation, and we are not giving up.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240613ek6d0038p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
