\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Despite the supreme court ruling for the  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pill, the battle is far from over\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 808 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 June 2024\par 19:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Justices unanimously upheld access to mifepristone, but Missouri, Idaho and Kansas are likely to revive the case\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jun/13/supreme-court-abortion-pill-access"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rejected an effort by anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  activists}}}\uc2  to roll back access to a common \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill \u8211\'20\'13 but the battle over the pill is far from over, both in the courts and in legislatures.\par \par \uc2 In a 9-0 unanimous decision on Thursday, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ruled that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists did not have standing, or the legal right to sue, over the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s (\uc2 FDA\uc2 ) regulation of mifepristone, one of two drugs typically used in a US medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But because the case turns on an interpretation of legal doctrine, the justices left open the possibility that someone else may challenge mifepristone in the future.\par \par \uc2 Judge Matthew Kacsmaryk, a federal judge in Texas who first heard arguments in the case, has allowed Missouri, Idaho and Kansas to join the case as plaintiffs. Although the supreme court declined to let those states join the case once it was before \uc2 Scotus\uc2 , the states may continue litigating it at a lower-court level and evade the standing defects that doomed the case\u8217\'20\'19s original plaintiffs.\par \par \uc2 The powerful anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, SBA Pro-Life America, already emphasized that possibility in the organization\u8217\'20\'19s statement on the Thursday ruling.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe pro-life states of Idaho, Kansas and Missouri are seeking to take up the challenge based on harms suffered by women in their states,\u8221\'20\'1D the group said.\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler, a professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2  who studies the legal history of reproduction, noted that \u8220\'20\'1Cthis ruling doesn\u8217\'20\'19t preclude anyone else from bringing the same claim.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUntil or unless the supreme court actually rejects these claims on the merits, they\u8217\'20\'19re not going anywhere,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 As medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now accounts for more than 60% of all US abortions, gutting access to it has become a major goal of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement in the years since the supreme court overturned {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates are not able to curb access to mifepristone through the courts, they may be able to do so through the White House. An administration headed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could, for example, try to direct the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to change its approach to mifepristone. The Thursday ruling, written by Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , seems to touch on this possibility.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe plaintiffs may present their concerns and objections to the President and \uc2 FDA\uc2  in the regulatory process, or to Congress and the President in the legislative process,\u8221\'20\'1D Kavanaugh wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd they may also express their views about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and mifepristone to fellow citizens, including in the political and electoral processes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Sepper, a professor at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2 , emphasized that \u8220\'20\'1Cthey don\u8217\'20\'19t mean \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think there\u8217\'20\'19s a very strong signal in the opinion that, well, if and when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins the presidency, head straight to the administration and ask them to change the approval of mifepristone,\u8221\'20\'1D she told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 Still, Nicole Huberfeld, a health law professor at \uc2 Boston University\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s school of public health, is not convinced such an effort would be successful. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not as if the president can just wave his hand and have things happen at the \uc2 FDA\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Huberfeld said. \u8220\'20\'1CYou can\u8217\'20\'19t always tell the scientists what you want them to say.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A future Trump administration could also target mifepristone using the Comstock Act, a 19th-century anti-vice law that, some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists argue, bans the mailing of all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials. The Biden administration has disagreed with that interpretation, issuing guidance arguing that the Comstock Act only bans such materials if the sender intends to use them to break the law, but a Trump administration may take a more hardline approach. Without involving Congress, Trump could use the Comstock Act to outlaw the mailing of mifepristone \u8211\'20\'13 which would result in a de facto ban on the pill.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/26/what-is-project-2025-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Project 2025}}}\uc2 , a playbook written by the influential conservative thinktank \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 , recommends that a future conservative administration do just that.\par \par \uc2 During oral arguments in the mifepristone case, both Justices \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the most conservative justices on the supreme court \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/27/supreme-court-abortion-pill-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brought up the Comstock Act}}}\uc2 . However, there was no mention of the law in either Thursday\u8217\'20\'19s majority opinion or a concurrence by Thomas.\par \par \uc2 Sepper suspects that the omission was a canny play by the conservative faction of the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s been concerns in the conservative legal movement that admitting Comstock would be revived in a Trump administration would be a poor political play. And so not mentioning Comstock at all is just wise strategy,\u8221\'20\'1D Sepper said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt certainly diverts attention from Comstock.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240613ek6d0030e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
