\par\par \uc2 Law\par \b \uc2 America braced as supreme court to hand down rulings on raft of key issues\b0\par\par\uc2 Ed Pilkington in New York \par \uc2 1,459 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 June 2024\par 19:14 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Justices to address \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , guns, social media \u8211\'20\'13 and whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can be prosecuted for role in January 6 insurrection\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/us-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US supreme court}}}\uc2  is poised to deliver a raft of politically sensitive decisions as it ends its judicial term, addressing tumultuous issues including whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can be prosecuted for his role in the January 6 insurrection in 2021, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for millions of women and the basic functioning of the federal government.\par \par \uc2 With the court entering its traditional June climax, observers are bracing themselves for yet another potentially seismic four weeks that could radically reshape American public life. Matters before the court include a possible loosening of gun laws in a country with already exceptionally lax controls, and new guardrails on how social media platforms deal with misinformation.\par \par \uc2 This will be the third full year under the new six-to-three supermajority of rightwing justices created by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s three appointments. How the court rules on the biggest cases will help cement its increasingly partisan and extremist reputation, against the backdrop of grating ethical scandals that call into question the justices\u8217\'20\'19 ability to deliver impartial judgments.\par \par \uc2 Top of the list of white-hot pending decisions are two cases relating to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s federal prosecution. They embroil the supreme court in the presidential election to a greater degree than at any time since Bush v Gore in 2000, which handed the White House to \uc2 George W Bush\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The justices have already allowed Trump to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/04/trump-scotus-colorado-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stay on the ballot}}}\uc2  by overturning a Colorado ruling that barred him from public office as an insurrectionist under the 14th amendment to the US constitution. Now the court must decide, in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scotusblog.com/case-files/cases/trump-v-united-states-3/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump v US}}}\uc2 , whether he can claim absolute presidential immunity from prosecution relating to the federal case in which he is charged with conspiracy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/03/trump-arraignment-indictment-jan-6-2020-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to subvert}}}\uc2  the 2020 election.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scotusblog.com/case-files/cases/fischer-v-united-states/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fischer v US}}}\uc2  concerns whether rioters at the US Capitol on 6 January 2021 can be charged under an obstruction statute. Should the justices rule against the use of that provision, as they indicated they would in oral arguments, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2024/apr/16/supreme-court-obstruction-trump-january-6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two of the four}}}\uc2  criminal charges facing Trump in the January 6 prosecution would fall.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis court has been called by some an imperial court that\u8217\'20\'19s willing to be brave in how it revisits precedent,\u8221\'20\'1D said Olatunde Johnson, a law professor at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , in New York. \u8220\'20\'1CIf the court issues an expansive ruling on immunity for a former president, that will raise questions about partisanship and ideology, and certainly influence the election.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s actions have been complicated by the recent revelations from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/05/28/us/justice-alito-neighbors-stop-steal-flag.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times}}}\uc2  that an upside-down flag seen as a symbol of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cstop the steal\u8221\'20\'1D conspiracy to overturn his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2020/nov/24/us-election-results-2020-joe-biden-defeats-donald-trump-to-win-presidency"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2020 defeat}}}\uc2  was flown at the Virginia home of rightwing justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  at the time of the Capitol insurrection. A second flag, also associated with the riot, was later flown at Alito and his wife\u8217\'20\'19s beach house in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt raises the question of whether Alito should have recused himself from these cases, and should still recuse himself from the ultimate decisions,\u8221\'20\'1D Johnson said. On Wednesday, Alito wrote to Democratic leaders in Congress {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/article/2024/may/29/samuel-alito-flag-jan-6-recusal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declining to recuse himself}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A less \u8220\'20\'1Cimperial\u8221\'20\'1D court might think that being thrown into the thick of the presidential election was quite enough judicial conflict for one year. Not this court.\par \par \uc2 The justices are also set to deliver opinions on the first \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases to reach them since they overturned the constitutional right to a termination {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in the Dobbs case}}}\uc2  two years ago, scrapping Roe v Wade. They are considering a challenge over access to mifepristone, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill that is now used in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/19/abortion-increase-2023-data"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 60%}}}\uc2  of US abortions.\par \par \uc2 Should the court side with the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups bringing the claim, some 57 million women of reproductive age living in states that allow abortions could be affected. Restrictions would also be further tightened for millions more women in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are already in place.\par \par \uc2 A second case, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scotusblog.com/case-files/cases/idaho-v-united-states/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Idaho v US}}}\uc2 , explores the clash between that state\u8217\'20\'19s strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and the federal law known as Emtala ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cms.gov/medicare/regulations-guidance/legislation/emergency-medical-treatment-labor-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Emergency Medical Treatment & Labor Act}}}\uc2  ), which obliges hospitals in receipt of federal funding to give patients emergency medical treatment, including abortions. The stakes here are also immense, as can be seen in Idaho, where the supreme court has allowed the ban to stand while it deliberates.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are horror stories of pregnant patients who have suffered horrible medical consequences such as unnecessary hysterectomies because they could not get emergency medical care under the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban,\u8221\'20\'1D said Maya Manian, a law professor and authority on reproductive rights at American University Washington College of Law.\par \par \uc2 For Manian, the confluence of the pair of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases underlines two critical truths: that the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement will not stop until it has achieved a total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban nationwide, and that by tearing up the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the supreme court has created a legal mess that is likely to dog it for years to come.\par \par \uc2 The court has also intervened in an area of settled law that for 40 years has ensured the smooth and effective operation of federal agencies. Since 1984, the courts have abided by the so-called \uc2 Chevron\uc2  doctrine, a principle introduced by the supreme court itself which says that judges should defer to the expertise of federal regulators when they reasonably interpret ambiguous statutes.\par \par \uc2 Such deference has been critical to the way that the federal government operates in core areas of public life, such as combatting the climate crisis or protecting labor rights.\par \par \uc2 Rightwing justices indicated in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scotusblog.com/2024/01/supreme-court-likely-to-discard-chevron/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 oral arguments}}}\uc2  that they will ditch \uc2 Chevron\uc2 , and with it curtail the regulatory powers of the administrative state. \u8220\'20\'1CIt would be the final nail in the coffin, weakening the ability of federal agencies to regulate over major areas,\u8221\'20\'1D Johnson said.\par \par \uc2 In any normal year, before any normal supreme court, the other cases that are pending in June would stand out in blazing headlines. As it is, they appear overshadowed.\par \par \uc2 Take the two cases relating to gun laws. It is testament to how far the rightwing supermajority has already torn down America\u8217\'20\'19s minimal gun controls that US v Rahimi is even being heard.\par \par \uc2 It concerns a challenge to an existing ban on firearms being possessed by those under domestic violence restraining orders. The individual bringing the case is a drug dealer who was placed under such an order after he abused his girlfriend in a parking lot, then shot at a bystander.\par \par \uc2 The justices have signaled that they are minded to let the domestic violence ban remain in place. But they were divided on the second gun case, which questions whether the Trump administration acted lawfully in banning so-called bump stocks \u8211\'20\'13 gadgets that allow semi-automatic rifles to fire almost as quickly as machine guns, at a rate of hundreds of rounds per minute.\par \par \uc2 The ban was introduced following the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/las-vegas-shooting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Las Vegas massacre}}}\uc2  in 2017, when a man opened fire at a country music festival, killing 60 people.\par \par \uc2 Social media is also on the docket as the court enters its end of term. No fewer than five cases came before the justices in three separate hearings, all within the same general territory of the collision between the first amendment right to free speech and the responsibility of social media companies and public officials in policing misinformation.\par \par \uc2 One of the cases has already been decided, with the court ruling that public officials can be sued for blocking their critics on social media, but only where the criticism relates to their official duties and not to their personal lives.\par \par \uc2 Two other big areas remain to be decided that could profoundly affect the way social media companies operate. The justices are wrestling with whether states should be able to proscribe the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/feb/26"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 editorial decisions}}}\uc2  of those businesses when they moderate content or remove users from their sites.\par \par \uc2 The case arose after Republican lawmakers in Florida and Texas imposed restrictions on social media companies on how they moderated content after Trump was taken off \uc2 Facebook\uc2  in response to his actions relating to the 6 January 2021 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-capitol-breach"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 attack on the US Capitol}}}\uc2 . The court is also pondering a move by Republican states, led by Louisiana and Missouri, to prevent the Biden administration from pressuring social media companies to remove misleading information from their platforms on subjects such as Covid and the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Rulings in these cases are likely to set parameters for the foreseeable future on how social media approaches the vexing question of where free speech ends and dangerous misinformation begins.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240611ek6a002pb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
