\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18They have no options\u8217\'20\'19: Texas court dims hope of timely  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  care for high-risk patients\b0\par\par\uc2 Mary Tuma \par \uc2 1,660 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 June 2024\par 12:40 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Kristen Anaya was told she must be on the cusp of death before doctors would give a life-saving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 After four rounds of in vitro fertilization, Kristen Anaya and her husband were elated to discover Anaya was finally pregnant \u8211\'20\'13 with a baby girl \u8211\'20\'13 last April. The 42-year-old Dallas-area woman called IVF a \u8220\'20\'1Clong and emotional journey\u8221\'20\'1D. Despite the cost and struggle, the process was well worth it for Anaya, who wanted to grow her family.\par \par \uc2 However, the good news would give way to an unexpectedly grueling and traumatic pregnancy that forced her to suffer for days before receiving care, due to Texas\u8217\'20\'19s severe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 At nearly 17 weeks pregnant, Anaya began experiencing heavy cramping and soon felt her water break. She was rushed to the hospital, where she continued to lose amniotic fluid. She developed a high fever and began shaking so uncontrollably that nurses had difficulty placing an IV. Anaya was in the early stages of sepsis, a potentially life-threatening condition.\par \par \uc2 Her OB-GYN explained that nothing could be done to save her daughter, whom Anaya had already named Tylee. Adding to Anaya\u8217\'20\'19s shock, the physician informed her that to stop the infection she needed immediate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, but because of Texas laws, she would have to \u8220\'20\'1Ckeep getting sicker\u8221\'20\'1D until the doctor could \u8220\'20\'1Cprove\u8221\'20\'1D to the hospital that her life was on the line.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI went from absolute terror, to sadness, to confusion all within minutes,\u8221\'20\'1D Anaya said. \u8220\'20\'1CI just learned that my child was not going to be born alive, and now the doctor is telling me I would have to put my life at risk before getting help. I went into complete shock. It was unbearable to process.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Anaya later joined a lawsuit asking the state to clarify when women in situations like hers can receive live-saving abortions, but last week, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.txcourts.gov/media/1458610/230629.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas supreme court }}}\uc2  rejected the suit. The all-Republican court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/31/texas-abortion-ban-medical-exceptions-supreme-court-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unanimously agreed}}}\uc2  the law is clear as-is.\par \par \uc2 Anaya\u8217\'20\'19s story underscores the immense struggle Texas patients with high-risk pregnancies \u8211\'20\'13 who have lived under strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans longer than other US residents \u8211\'20\'13 have faced for more than two years. Without clear medical exemptions, the laws have tied the hands of doctors and hospitals who deeply fear the punitive consequences, placing patient care in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 But it\u8217\'20\'19s not just high-risk patients bearing the brunt of Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. Over the last decade, Texas has been a national leader in enacting onerous {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2017/jan/19/texas-abortion-law-hb2-access-impact-study"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  restrictions}}}\uc2 , passing the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/01/texas-abortion-law-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most extreme}}}\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in the US well before the fall of Roe.\par \par \uc2 State leaders, who have edged even {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2024/03/18/1239283038/how-the-texas-gop-has-grown-more-and-more-conservative"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 closer to the far right}}}\uc2  in recent years, don\u8217\'20\'19t appear to be slowing down. The Texas Republican party {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/may/30/texas-republicans-vote-death-penalty-abortion-providers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 is considering}}}\uc2  a proposal that would threaten doctors who violate the law with the death penalty. Lawmakers have also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/legal/legalindustry/texas-lawmakers-target-law-firms-aiding-abortion-access-2022-07-08/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shown interest}}}\uc2  in targeting out-of-state travel for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, a movement that has already gained {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/mar/09/pastor-push-national-abortion-ban-sanctuary-cities-for-the-unborn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 traction in Texas}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat happens with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in Texas doesn\u8217\'20\'19t stay in Texas,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rewirenewsgroup.com/authors/jessica-mason-pieklo/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 writes}}}\uc2  Jessica Mason Pieklo with the reproductive rights outlet Rewire. \u8220\'20\'1CIn fact, it informs \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy nationwide.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , president of the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2  (CRR), which brought the lawsuit, called the ruling \u8220\'20\'1Cdeeply offensive\u8221\'20\'1D to the women they represent. During an emotional press call, several plaintiffs expressed feeling \u8220\'20\'1Cignored\u8221\'20\'1D and re-traumatized by the ruling, and said they were \u8220\'20\'1Cterrified\u8221\'20\'1D for women in Texas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe all deserve bodily autonomy. Every day, people in Texas are being told that they have no options,\u8221\'20\'1D said Amanda Zurawski, the cases\u8217\'20\'19s lead plaintiff, who nearly died after pregnancy complications.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18An incredibly tough situation to put doctors in\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Apart from the devastating effect that Texas\u8217\'20\'19s ban has had on women needing abortions, the toll on doctors has been significant.\par \par \uc2 Since Texas\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/01/texas-abortion-law-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 near-total \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  took effect in 2021, the year before Roe v Wade was overturned, reports began emerging of doctors denying or delaying \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in high-risk or emergency situations, in some cases pushing some patients to \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jul/13/texas-abortion-ban-maternal-health-risk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 death\u8217\'20\'19s door}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D before intervening. After Roe was overturned, another \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban went into effect, threatening already wary doctors with the loss of their medical license, $100,000 in fines, and up to life in prison for violating the law.\par \par \uc2 While Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law carves out exceptions for \u8220\'20\'1Crisk of death or a substantial impairment of a major bodily function\u8221\'20\'1D, Anaya and plaintiffs say those exemptions are ambiguous, causing physicians to refuse care rather than risk harsh criminal and civil punishment. Over a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/20/texas-women-pregnancy-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two-day trial}}}\uc2  in July, Texas women recounted stories of being denied \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care despite life-threatening pregnancy complications, including hemorrhaging and sepsis. Kate Cox, a Texas woman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/05/texas-woman-emergency-abortion-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 denied an emergency \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  for her non-viable pregnancy whose separate case attracted national attention, only reinforced the need for clarity.\par \par \uc2 While the Texas court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling offers some subtle clarifications, including raising the burden on state prosecutors wishing to criminally enforce the ban on doctors and allowing abortions in cases of premature rupture of membranes, the decision overall largely maintains the \u8220\'20\'1Cstatus quo\u8221\'20\'1D, Liz Sepper, a \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2  law school professor who focuses on reproductive healthcare law, said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe bottom line is that the Texas supreme court ruling basically puts us in the same situation today as we were yesterday, leaving pregnant patients at risk in emergency situations \u8211\'20\'13 and now without hope that there will be meaningful change through the courts,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sepper.\par \par \uc2 In addition to fearing severe criminal penalties from Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, doctors and hospitals also lack the safety net of federal protections. Since 2022, Texas officials have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/jan/02/texas-emergency-abortions-ban-despite-federal-guidance"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 successfully fought against}}}\uc2  the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s guidance that the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cms.gov/medicare/regulations-guidance/legislation/emergency-medical-treatment-labor-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act}}}\uc2  (Emtala), which requires hospitals to provide medically necessary care to stabilize patients, includes emergency abortions. Texas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texasattorneygeneral.gov/news/releases/paxton-secures-victory-against-biden-administration-blocks-hhs-forcing-healthcare-providers-perform"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 argues}}}\uc2  that hospitals are not immune from penalties if they perform abortions as part of stabilizing treatment. A ruling on whether Emtala supersedes state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law is expected this month from the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dr John Thoppil, an Austin-based OB-GYN and board member of the Texas Association of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, says the Texas court failed to help doctors navigate the stringent law and they remain fearful for both their patients and their own livelihoods.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think a lot of us [physicians] feel dejected,\u8221\'20\'1D Thoppil said. \u8220\'20\'1CFor months, we were hoping for more clarity from the state, but we didn\u8217\'20\'19t get it. Instead, we are being forced to continue to carry this huge burden of balancing our job to care for patients with complications against the threat of prosecution and life in jail \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s an incredibly tough situation to put doctors in.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Women are suffering\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 With no choice but to wait for her health to deteriorate, Anaya\u8217\'20\'19s doctor was forced to \u8220\'20\'1Cbuild a case\u8221\'20\'1D that her life was in danger to persuade the hospital\u8217\'20\'19s termination committee to approve her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Every three hours, Anaya underwent tests. Her doctor presented her white blood count and lactic acid levels, and her fetus\u8217\'20\'19s heartbeat, to hospital administration, who said she was not yet sick enough. Anaya recalls her doctor practically \u8220\'20\'1Cbegging\u8221\'20\'1D the hospital to act. Meanwhile, her fever steadily rose \u8211\'20\'13 she was freezing, shaking intensely and constantly vomiting.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was stuck in this horrific limbo, just waiting to get worse and worse,\u8221\'20\'1D said Anaya. \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t even describe the amount of anxiety I felt. It was a living nightmare.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After nearly 24 painful hours, Anaya\u8217\'20\'19s blood tests reached such dangerous levels that hospital administration finally approved the procedure. Due to her septic infection, Anaya battled for her health in the hospital for five more days, undergoing two blood transfusions. Eventually, she was released and said she spent the next few weeks \u8220\'20\'1Ccrying on the couch\u8221\'20\'1D with grief.\par \par \uc2 During court proceedings, Texas attorneys argued that the onus is on the individual doctor to determine whether a high-risk patient qualifies for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not on the law itself. But Anaya doesn\u8217\'20\'19t place blame on the medical staff who treated her. She has sympathy for doctors, who she says \u8220\'20\'1Cclearly have their hands tied\u8221\'20\'1D by Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe doctors and nurses looked so helpless and defeated. There was so much sadness in the room \u8211\'20\'13 we both understood they were trying their best and wanted to help, but couldn\u8217\'20\'19t,\u8221\'20\'1D said Anaya. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not angry at them \u8211\'20\'13 I\u8217\'20\'19m angry at the laws created by the state.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Marc Hearron, an attorney with CRR says that \u8220\'20\'1Call eyes should be on the Texas medical board\u8221\'20\'1D, the state agency that regulates medical practice in Texas.\par \par \uc2 In January, the TMB finally took action after a pair of Austin attorneys filed a petition pushing the agency to consider issuing guidance on the law\u8217\'20\'19s exceptions. However, the TMB\u8217\'20\'19s proposal has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2024/03/22/texas-medical-exception-board-abortion-guidance/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disappointed reproductive rights advocates}}}\uc2 , including plaintiffs in the Zurawski case, who hoped the board would offer a specific list of conditions covered by the law. In fact, many of the doctors, lawyers and experts who testified during the board\u8217\'20\'19s May meeting worried that the new rules would actually create {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2024/05/20/texas-medical-board-abortion-guidance/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more obstacles}}}\uc2  to patient care, as they would force additional reporting requirements on physicians. The rule-making process is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://forms.office.com/pages/responsepage.aspx?id=O894vflvjkOlKoDWloMTsTz93wATleVOiZazwD363OpUNjkxRklERzA0TVlYSVpHMkFFQ04xUE9CSS4u"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ongoing}}}\uc2  and probably will not be finalized before the end of June.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, patients like Anaya will continue to suffer the harrowing consequences of the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t stress how important it is that we have clear exceptions to the law,\u8221\'20\'1D said Anaya. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to empower doctors to feel confident in treating patients like me, who could have died, before it\u8217\'20\'19s too late. We need them to not feel afraid to help. Women are suffering and they are going to keep suffering.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240608ek68001ba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
