\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 US state  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban exemptions aren\u8217\'20\'19t vague by accident. Uncertainty is the point\b0\par\par\uc2 Judith Levine \par \uc2 988 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 June 2024\par 13:09 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Anti-choice statutes are designed to keep health providers fearful of running afoul of the law. Women suffer for it\par \par \uc2 Anyone who has lived under the control of an abusive partner or parent knows that the problem is not just what\u8217\'20\'19s prohibited. It\u8217\'20\'19s what you\u8217\'20\'19re unsure is prohibited. The prospect of punishment instills fear. Vagueness about what will be punished promotes caution. Just in case, the teenager doesn\u8217\'20\'19t hang out with certain friends. The teacher deletes the controversial book from the curriculum.\par \par \uc2 The doctor decides not to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the patient\u8217\'20\'19s health or life is at risk, but, maybe, not imminently so.\par \par \uc2 This is what is happening across the 21 states that have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in all but the direst of circumstances. It\u8217\'20\'19s what happened to Amanda Zurawski, an Austin, Texas, resident who learned at 18 weeks\u8217\'20\'19 gestation that her cervix was prematurely dilated, spelling certain death for her fetus, already named Willow, and posing a grave threat to her own health.\par \par \uc2 But because there was still fetal cardiac activity, the doctors sent Zurawski home to get sick enough to qualify for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under Texas\u8217\'20\'19s ban. The law permits the procedure only when a patient would otherwise lose \u8220\'20\'1Cmajor bodily function\u8221\'20\'1D or die. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t say when that might be. And it makes no allowance for a fatal fetal anomaly.\par \par \uc2 Zurawski went into sepsis \u8211\'20\'13 full-body infection \u8211\'20\'13 and spent three days in the ICU. She survived, but it\u8217\'20\'19s unlikely that her fertility did.\par \par \uc2 In Zurawski v Texas (2023), the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2  (CRR) argued on behalf of two doctors and 20 patients that Texas\u8217\'20\'19s statute was so vaguely written that providers could not know whether they were breaking the law \u8211\'20\'13 risking penalties as severe as imprisonment \u8211\'20\'13 if they did the medically correct thing. The CRR called this failure a violation of the women\u8217\'20\'19s constitutional right to life.\par \par \uc2 In December 2023, a lower court judge enjoined the law awaiting further clarification and permitted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the doctor\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cgood-faith judgment\u8221\'20\'1D deemed it necessary. \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , Texas\u8217\'20\'19s fanatically anti-choice attorney general, appealed the injunction to the Texas supreme court. And last week that court removed the injunction, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/Zurawski-v-Texas-SCOTX-Opinion.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruling}}}\uc2  that the law is perfectly clear.\par \par \uc2 The Human Life Protection Act \u8220\'20\'1Cpermits a physician to intervene to address a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life-threatening physical condition before death or serious physical impairment are imminent\u8221\'20\'1D, wrote Justice Jane Bland. \u8220\'20\'1CA physician who tells a patient, \u8216\'20\'18Your life is threatened by a complication that has arisen during your pregnancy, and you may die, or there is a serious risk you will suffer substantial physical impairment unless an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is performed,\u8217\'20\'19 and in the same breath states \u8216\'20\'18but the law won\u8217\'20\'19t allow me to provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in these circumstances\u8217\'20\'19 is simply wrong in that legal assessment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Why don\u8217\'20\'19t lawmakers clarify their statutes? For one thing, they can\u8217\'20\'19t. Most of them wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t know a fallopian tube from a brake fluid line\par \par \uc2 In other words, if the patient is harmed, it is the doctor\u8217\'20\'19s fault, not the law\u8217\'20\'19s.\par \par \uc2 Zurawski v Texas was the first lawsuit to challenge a ban on behalf of women with complicated pregnancies since the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade in June 2022. There are certain to be more. At the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.help.senate.gov/hearings/the-assault-on-womens-freedoms-how-abortion-bans-have-created-a-health-care-nightmare-across-america?utm_source=substack&utm_medium=email"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 4 June hearing}}}\uc2  of the Democrat-led Senate committee on health, education, labor, and pensions, witnesses gave evidence of what the committee called a national \u8220\'20\'1Chealthcare nightmare\u8221\'20\'1D caused by the bans and the confusion they\u8217\'20\'19re wreaking. In a few places, such as Tennessee, elected officials have suggested that legislators amend the laws to make them easier to understand. But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-exception-lawsuit-legislature-confusion-b2808df90937e96887aa4e1f9c565771"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 few such proposals}}}\uc2  have been filed.\par \par \uc2 In its lawsuit, the CRR asked Texas to tighten the statute. But the justices did not, and did not instruct the legislature to do so. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are right back where we started,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.houstonchronicle.com/politics/texas/article/emergency-abortion-supreme-court-19488178.php"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  an incensed Zurawski.\par \par \uc2 They may be even further back than that. At a virtual {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2024/05/20/texas-medical-board-abortion-guidance/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hearing}}}\uc2  10 days before the ruling, members of the Texas medical board argued with lawyers and healthcare providers \u8211\'20\'13 and with each other \u8211\'20\'13 over interpretations of its guidance written to clear up ambiguities in the law. But all the new guidelines did was add a requirement that providers submit profuse documentation justifying their decisions. After the five-hour hearing and hundreds of written comments, everyone agreed on one thing: the irresolution is now worse.\par \par \uc2 Why don\u8217\'20\'19t lawmakers clarify their statutes? For one thing, they can\u8217\'20\'19t. Most of them wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t know a fallopian tube from a brake fluid line, and even if they did, there\u8217\'20\'19s no way to codify everything that can possibly go wrong in a problem pregnancy or every medical decision that must be made in response. The Texas court said the doctor who opts against an emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes the wrong legal assessment. But if most legislators are not doctors, few doctors are also lawyers.\par \par \uc2 Yet the CRR\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to push the state to amend a bad law to make it a little less bad is the same tactic that the pro-choice legal establishment pursued throughout the Roe years \u8211\'20\'13 and look where that got us.\par \par \uc2 The tactic is not just counterproductive. It\u8217\'20\'19s delusional. The laws\u8217\'20\'19 vagueness is not an oversight, not the result of sloppy or ignorant drafting. Vagueness is the intention. Along with police, prosecutors and civilian vigilantes enforcing the laws, the states are counting on self-policing. They may even hope that providers do less than they are legally permitted.\par \par \uc2 And if bad things happen \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cpainful\u8221\'20\'1D as that might be, admitted Bland \u8211\'20\'13 the state can duck the blame.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Judith Levine is a Brooklyn journalist and essayist, a contributing writer to the Intercept and the author of five books\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240607ek67001e6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
