\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Senate Republicans block bill to recognize legal right to contraception\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,110 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 June 2024\par 23:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As expected, Right to Contraception act falls short of garnering the 60 votes Senate Democrats needed to advance the bill\par \par \uc2 Senate Republicans on Wednesday blocked a bill that would have recognized a legal right to contraception, weeks after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made \u8211\'20\'13 and quickly walked back \u8211\'20\'13 comments indicating he was willing to restrict access to birth control.\par \par \uc2 As expected, the Right to Contraception act fell short of garnering the 60 votes Senate Democrats needed to advance the bill. Before the vote, Democrats in effect acknowledged that they were daring Republicans to go on the record opposing the right to something that almost all American women use at some point in their lifetime.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSenate Republicans just went on the record saying they do NOT support access to contraception,\u8221\'20\'1D the Democratic US senator for Washington state, \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2 , posted on X after the vote. \u8220\'20\'1CYes, you read that right.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Republicans argued that the legislation was unnecessary because, they say, contraceptives are not at political risk. In a Senate speech Wednesday, the Republican Iowa senator \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2  accused Democrats of \u8220\'20\'1Cfear-mongering in the name of politics\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Several Republican senators, including \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina, Katie Britt of Alabama, JD Vance of Ohio and \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  of Utah, did not vote. Republicans \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  of Maine and \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  of Alaska, who have previously broken with their party to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, voted in favor of the Right to Contraception act.\par \par \uc2 The Wednesday vote was part of an offensive broadside by Democrats to spotlight their work around reproductive rights, a vital issue in the upcoming 2024 elections, nearly two years after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, including a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.duckworth.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/Right%20to%20IVF%20Act%20-%20Summary.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sweeping package of legislation}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Trump backtracked on comments in a May interview that he was \u8220\'20\'1Clooking at\u8221\'20\'1D restricting access to contraceptives, claiming on Truth Social that his own words were \u8220\'20\'1Ca Democrat-fabricated lie\u8221\'20\'1D. Almost 90% of voters say Americans should have the right to make decisions about contraception without government interference, according to recent polling from the progressive firm Impact Research.\par \par \uc2 Efforts to codify the legal right to contraception largely stalled in 2024. So far this year, legislators in at least 27 states have introduced more than 50 bills and proposed constitutional amendments to protect the right to contraception, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , which tracks and supports reproductive rights. But only one of those states, Virginia, passed that legislation and handed it off to the governor\u8217\'20\'19s mansion. \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2 , the Republican governor, vetoed it.\par \par \uc2 Redefining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  beliefs are responsible for much of the opposition to birth-control protections.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou won\u8217\'20\'19t find a lot of Republicans who say they oppose birth control. You will find Republicans who don\u8217\'20\'19t want right-to-contraception bills,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mary Ziegler, a \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2  law professor who studies the legal history of reproduction. \u8220\'20\'1CThe reasons for that are complicated. But probably the most important is that a lot of Republicans think that common contraceptives are abortifacients.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has quietly worked to redefine the meaning of \u8220\'20\'1Cabortifacient\u8221\'20\'1D, or drugs that induce abortions, for years. In 2014, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  permitted \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2 , a Christian-owned chain of craft stores, to avoid covering their employees\u8217\'20\'19 morning-after pills and intrauterine devices (IUDs) because their religious beliefs deemed them abortifacients \u8211\'20\'13 even though scientists disagree. Morning-after pills and IUDs can prevent pregnancy through a variety of mechanisms, but they ultimately can only keep pregnancies from starting, not disrupt existing ones.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025, a playbook written for a potential second Trump administration by the influential conservative thinktank \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 , recommends that the federal government drop a requirement that insurers cover Ella, an emergency contraceptive pill, which it deems an \u8220\'20\'1Cabortifacient\u8221\'20\'1D. Meanwhile, the prominent anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Students for Life lists birth-control pills, IUDs and several other forms of hormonal birth control as \u8220\'20\'1Cabortifacients\u8221\'20\'1D on its website.\par \par \uc2 Ziegler said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents could even use existing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans to outlaw contraception. After Roe fell, three states \u8211\'20\'13 Georgia, Utah and Oklahoma \u8211\'20\'13 changed their legal definitions of \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D to remove language that protected contraception, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deliverypdf.ssrn.com/delivery.php?ID=077084099072012071107093123117031092029025017074071027094026120076084102007088124074114057025013024031019077015102022066001005018023002029065068026105012119066095070019081125017067087089030009017076090093030114027023104022065098080026125024098123104&EXT=pdf&INDEX=TRUE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a draft of a forthcoming paper}}}\uc2  by Greer Donley, an associate professor at the University of Pittsburgh School of Law, and law student Caroline Kelly.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is no longer a fringe, side issue for a lot of these groups,\u8221\'20\'1D Ziegler said of redefining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a central plank of what they\u8217\'20\'19re trying to do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 also recommends that the federal government require insurers to cover \u8220\'20\'1Cfertility awareness\u8211\'20\'13based\u8221\'20\'1D birth-control methods. These non-hormonal methods often involve avoiding sex or using condoms on certain days of the month, and tend to be between 75% and 88% effective at preventing pregnancy, according to the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 . IUDs are more than 99% effective.\par \par \uc2 In 2024, legislators in five states introduced bills that specifically sought to restrict minors\u8217\'20\'19 ability to access contraception, such as by mandating that physicians tell minor patients\u8217\'20\'19 parents about their birth-control prescriptions. None of these bills advanced far in their state legislatures.\par \par \uc2 But in one case, litigation has succeeded where legislation has faltered. In March, the US court of appeals for the fifth circuit upheld a ruling that found Texas providers in the federal Title X network, the nation\u8217\'20\'19s largest family-planning program, must obtain parental consent before they can dispense birth control to minors. Title X dates back to the 1970s and aims to provide free or subsidized contraception, STD screenings and other family-planning services to people regardless of age.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s first presidency took an axe to family-planning funding by writing new rules that forced almost one-third of clinics that received grants funding under Title X to leave the program. That left more than 1.5 million people without access to Title X-funded care, including its contraception services.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the White House, he rolled back Trump\u8217\'20\'19s changes to Title X. But Project 2025 suggests the federal government revive them. Title X, the playbook adds, should be \u8220\'20\'1Creframed with a focus on better education around fertility awareness and holistic family planning\u8221\'20\'1D, while clinics it funds should stress the \u8220\'20\'1Cimportance of marriage\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI do feel like birth-control access is being chipped away at, much the same way that Roe was,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kelsey Grimes, senior counsel at the \uc2 National Women\u8217\'20\'19s Law Center\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CBut I think that people \u8211\'20\'13 and by people, I mean legislators and the general public to some degree \u8211\'20\'13 don\u8217\'20\'19t really believe that birth control is threatened the same way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240605ek65001eb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
