\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Texas supreme court rejects challenge to  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban over medical exceptions\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 825 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 May 2024\par 20:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Court rules unanimously on challenge from over 20 women who say they were denied medically necessary abortions\par \par \uc2 The Texas supreme court on Friday rejected a challenge from more than 20 women who said they were denied medically necessary abortions under the state\u8217\'20\'19s near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, ruling in an unanimous opinion that the state\u8217\'20\'19s ban can stand as is.\par \par \uc2 Two doctors had also joined the women\u8217\'20\'19s case, which was the first post-Roe v Wade lawsuit to be filed by women who said that their health, lives or fertility had been endangered by abortions bans. Lawsuits filed by women in other states with near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, including Idaho and Tennessee, soon followed.\par \par \uc2 The case did not seek to overturn Texas\u8217\'20\'19s near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, but to amend it to clarify when medically necessary abortions can be performed.\par \par \uc2 Although every US \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban technically permits abortions in medical emergencies, doctors in Texas and across the country have said that the wording of the bans \u8211\'20\'13 penned by politicians rather than medical professionals \u8211\'20\'13 is too vague and confusing to be workable in practice. Instead, these doctors say, they have been forced to wait and watch until patients get sick enough to intervene.\par \par \uc2 In its ruling, the Republican-dominated Texas supreme court disagreed with those doctors. \u8220\'20\'1CTexas law permits a life-saving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote Justice Jane Bland, who authored the court\u8217\'20\'19s opinion.\par \par \uc2 Bland continued: \u8220\'20\'1CA physician who tells a patient, \u8216\'20\'18Your life is threatened by a complication that has arisen during your pregnancy, and you may die, or there is a serious risk you will suffer substantial physical impairment unless an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is performed,\u8217\'20\'19 and in the same breath states \u8216\'20\'18but the law won\u8217\'20\'19t allow me to provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in these circumstances\u8217\'20\'19 is simply wrong in that legal assessment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The woman who lent her name to the case, Amanda Zurawski, was 18 weeks into a much-wanted pregnancy when her water broke early \u8211\'20\'13 a development that doomed her chances of delivering a healthy baby. Although continuing the pregnancy posed significant risks to Zurawski\u8217\'20\'19s health, her doctors said that they could not perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under Texas law because the fetus still had cardiac activity. Zurawski ended up developing sepsis and spending days in the intensive care unit.\par \par \uc2 Other women who sued Texas said they were denied abortions even after their pregnancies were diagnosed with a litany of lethal conditions that would leave their fetuses with underdeveloped brains, skulls and other organs. Several fled the state for out-of-state abortions. One woman, Samantha Casiano, stayed in Texas and gave birth to a daughter, Halo, who struggled for air and died hours after birth. When Casiano recounted her experience in testimony last year, she threw up on the stand.\par \par \uc2 Lauren Miller, another plaintiff in the case, was pregnant with twins when she learned one of the fetuses had severe abnormalities and would probably never make it to birth. Her pregnancy caused Miller to throw up uncontrollably, sending her to the emergency room twice. Despite the risks to her health, her doctors did not suggest an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Miller ultimately received a fetal reduction, a procedure where doctors abort one fetus, in Colorado. She gave birth to a healthy son.\par \par \uc2 When she read the Texas court\u8217\'20\'19s opinion on Friday, Miller was shocked by how little she and the other women were mentioned.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI read it this morning. And then I reread it because I felt like something was missing. It was when I reread it and saw it the second time, I was like: \u8216\'20\'18 We\u8217\'20\'19re missing. We are erased from our own case,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Miller said. She said that, after reading the ruling, she gave her young son a hug. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was just registration, once again, about how little not just my life mattered, but how little his life mattered to the Texas supreme court.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Last year, a Travis county judge had issued a court order in the case that allowed doctors to perform abortions if they determined through \u8220\'20\'1Cgood faith judgment\u8221\'20\'1D that the procedure was necessary.\par \par \uc2 That order was immediately put on hold due to an appeal by the Texas attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s office. Friday\u8217\'20\'19s order vacates the order completely.\par \par \uc2 Although the Friday ruling will not legally affect the lawsuits in other states, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/28/texas-supreme-court-hears-extreme-abortion-ban-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 advocates had hoped that a favorable ruling}}}\uc2  from the Texas state supreme court could help, because the medical exemptions in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are often written with very similar language.\par \par \uc2 In light of Friday\u8217\'20\'19s ruling, Miller urged people to vote for candidates who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights come November. Multiple members of the Texas supreme court are up for re-election this year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m going to be sad today. I\u8217\'20\'19m going to be bummed. But then we\u8217\'20\'19re gonna have a weekend,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not done fighting.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240531ek5v0028v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
