\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We don\u8217\'20\'19t have a democracy\u8217\'20\'19: why some Oregonians want to join Idaho\b0\par\par\uc2 Kiran Moodley in Prineville, Oregon \par \uc2 1,753 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 May 2024\par 22:23 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Proponents of the Greater Idaho movement have argued Democrats in Portland don\u8217\'20\'19t understand their way of life\par \par \uc2 Under a large tent at the Crook county fairgrounds in Prineville, Oregon, six people stand in a neat line, each clutching the gun in their holster. \u8220\'20\'1CShooters, set,\u8221\'20\'1D a man to the side yells. They wait. A light turns on in the centre of the target. They fire. A clock above records how long it took them to draw, shoot and, if they managed to, hit the target. They\u8217\'20\'19re playing in pairs. Best two out of three wins.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Oregon\u8217\'20\'19s Cowboy Fast Draw State Championship, a sport organisers say is \u8220\'20\'1Cdedicated to the romance and legend of the Old West\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The residents of Prineville are voting on 21 May on a fundamental question: \u8220\'20\'1CShould Crook county represent that its citizens support efforts to move the Idaho state border to include Crook county?\u8221\'20\'1D If a majority votes yes, the county will become the 13th to vote in favor of leaving the state of Oregon and joining next-door Idaho instead. Polarisation breeds frustration which creates secession. America\u8217\'20\'19s past and present.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Calvin Foster, who competed under the name of Scrub Brush, is the man in charge of affairs at the Fast Draw, and he sums up his political frustration.\par \par \uc2 The cities \u8220\'20\'1Cdon\u8217\'20\'19t understand the life that we have out on this side of the state\u8221\'20\'1D, he says.\par \par \uc2 The majority of Oregon\u8217\'20\'19s just over 4 million residents live on the western side of the Cascade Mountains, which run down the centre of the state. To the west lie high-density cities like Portland, Salem and Eugene, which in past years have voted largely Democratic. To the east are sparsely populated counties that have reliably voted Republican. Democrats have held the governorship since 1987.\par \par \uc2 The presidency of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Covid pandemic have heightened divisions \u8211\'20\'13 with different groups starkly diverging on how they think the state should move forward. Crook county voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , a Republican governor, against decriminalising drugs and against restrictions on gun ownership. The state went the other way every time.\par \par \uc2 Foster explains what he sees as the difference between west and east: that the culture out here is about family and guns. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve grown up with guns, been shooting guns since I was probably five,\u8221\'20\'1D he said as we sat on the bleachers and watched the competition. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a right that we\u8217\'20\'19ve had and hopefully we keep forever.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That life is one of farmers and ranchers, said Jim Bunch (competing as Jabberin\u8217\'20\'19 Jim, a nickname his wife chose), a livelihood that he says city folk don\u8217\'20\'19t appreciate. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople that think that livestock is bad, that cutting timber is bad, that farming is bad. They want to get rid of agriculture. They want to get rid of us being able to control our own lives.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 People here believe the other side is forcing their ideals on them. One thing that comes up again and again is not just \u8220\'20\'1Cfamily\u8221\'20\'1D values, a nod to Oregon\u8217\'20\'19s progressive stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and LGBTQ+ rights, but the green economy. Foster said electric cars were what really irk him. Larry Lansdowne, a shooter from Idaho, said he understood his neighbours\u8217\'20\'19 frustration.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe really don\u8217\'20\'19t want you to come here and start telling us about why you can\u8217\'20\'19t carry that gun or you need to drive an electric car,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve been living this lifestyle for hundreds of years and we really just don\u8217\'20\'19t want to change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The most extreme stance I heard was from Bunch. \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t have a democracy, we are a constitutional republic,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. I asked him what he meant by that distinction.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have a constitution that lays down the laws for us. As a republic, the individual is protected. So the minority can be protected. It\u8217\'20\'19s not just majority rules.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo you feel at the moment that democracy, especially within Oregon, isn\u8217\'20\'19t working for you?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COh, democracy doesn\u8217\'20\'19t work,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, emphatically.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 If democracy does not work for supporters of annexation, they are pursuing democratic means to change it.\par \par \uc2 The Greater Idaho movement was set up in 2019 and has campaigned to put its measures on the ballot. Its current proposal would see 14 counties move states, along with sections of three others. Originally, the plan included five more counties in south-western Oregon, but after two voted against the proposal, the movement scaled back its ambitions.\par \par \uc2 Moving the state lines is a tall order, given that both Oregon and Idaho legislatures would have to agree, along with the respective governors, and then for Congress to approve the matter. But the movement argues history shows this can happen. West Virginia was formed after separating from Virginia in 1863, and Maine was created by cutting itself off from Massachusetts in 1820.\par \par \uc2 I spoke with Matt McCaw, the group\u8217\'20\'19s executive director. He and his wife lived in Portland, Oregon\u8217\'20\'19s largest city, for 20 years before moving east because on \u8220\'20\'1Calmost every issue\u8221\'20\'1D, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , LGBTQ+, guns, drugs, McCaw was opposed to the progressive measures enacted by state legislators. He said that while there had always been this urban-rural divide, it had become worse recently.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur whole country got more polarised with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D he said as we chatted at Smith Rock State Park. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd then Covid just drove a wedge through all of it. Oregon was very heavy on lockdowns. They closed schools. They forced masks on people. People in western Oregon wanted that. The people in eastern Oregon were opposed to those policies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 McCaw and his wife are evangelical Christians, and faith is \u8220\'20\'1Cfirst and foremost in every decision we make\u8221\'20\'1D, he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey were telling us, \u8216\'20\'18You can\u8217\'20\'19t go to church.\u8217\'20\'19 Never in my wildest dreams would I have thought that my government would say, \u8216\'20\'18You can\u8217\'20\'19t go to church.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D He said Covid showed him you need a government that aligns with your values. Idaho, McCaw said, fits that bill.\par \par \uc2 The problem, I put to McCaw, is that this would lead to a nation where democracy is redundant. Everyone would split into their Democratic and Republican states and just stick with it. The divided states of America.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re saying we shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t divide up, because that will cause things to be more polarised. But if you leave these people together, it\u8217\'20\'19s not going to change. The two cultures are drifting further apart and want different things. If you continue to force people that want polar opposite things together, that is not a productive or healthy way to run a society.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 In a flip of the state-wide picture, it is progressives who are in the minority in Crook county. Outside the courthouse one Sunday, I chatted with Priscilla Smith, chair of the county\u8217\'20\'19s Democrats, who was leading a small rally against the Greater Idaho movement. About 20 people were holding banners that said \u8220\'20\'1CWe Love Oregon \u8230\'20\'26 all of it\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1COregon, Yes, IdaNo\u8221\'20\'1D. One person was wearing a pro-Ukraine T-shirt.\par \par \uc2 For Smith, this is also about family values. \u8220\'20\'1CMy concern is for my granddaughters,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, wearing a bright blue hoodie bearing Oregon\u8217\'20\'19s state seal. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat happens to their health if we become part of Idaho?\u8221\'20\'1D Idaho has a near-total ban on abortions.\par \par \uc2 One sign at the protest read \u8220\'20\'1COregon values are my values.\u8221\'20\'1D I asked Priscilla what those were. \u8220\'20\'1CMy values are that we treat everyone equally. Especially because I have a trans grandson. That we care about everyone. The proponents [of Greater Idaho] think our values in this part of the state align more closely with Idaho. Well, mine don\u8217\'20\'19t. So they don\u8217\'20\'19t speak for everyone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Tom Andersen a Democratic, represents a district on the western side in the state legislature. I asked him whether his party was at fault for allowing a movement like Greater Idaho to emerge.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think fault is a pretty strong word, but I say part of the responsibility is that the Democrats have not listened to the other side,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe could do better. We need to listen to them. They feel that their needs have not been addressed by the whole state of Oregon.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Andersen warned that while it was worth engaging with the Greater Idaho movement, the idea of splitting the state was a concern, given it may send a message to other states to do the same. \u8220\'20\'1CThat would open a Pandora\u8217\'20\'19s box, a slippery slope,\u8221\'20\'1D Andersen said, and one that could lead to a situation where \u8220\'20\'1Cdemocracy does fall apart\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDear Father, we thank you for the opportunity and the freedom in this country to come together and to discuss issues like this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mike McCarter, the president of the Greater Idaho movement, was leading a prayer at the start of a question-and-answer session hosted by McCaw at the Crook county library in Prineville. About two dozen people turned up. On one side, a few men sat silently with \u8220\'20\'1CTrump\u8221\'20\'1D hats on. Across the aisle sat Priscilla Smith alongside some people from the rally.\par \par \uc2 She took the microphone. \u8220\'20\'1CMy first concern is the fact that Idaho has one of the strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights laws in the nation,\u8221\'20\'1D Smith asked, adding, \u8220\'20\'1CThe other thing I have a real angst about with Idaho is their position on LGBTQ. I have a grandchild who\u8217\'20\'19s trans. How is their life going to be affected?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 McCaw and Smith then bickered over the details of Oregon\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, before McCaw summarised: \u8220\'20\'1CThe bigger point of all that is that people have very different, very strong opinions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And the same thing is true with trans kids.\u8221\'20\'1D He said these were the two hottest topics that spoke to the great divide.\par \par \uc2 After that, the debate was cordial. One older man in a cowboy hat did call the politicians in Salem \u8220\'20\'1Cheathens\u8221\'20\'1D. Yet there were no heated back-and-forths, just a few mutters and murmurs in agreement or disagreement.\par \par \uc2 As people began to get up and leave amid a smattering of applause, McCaw ended the proceedings: \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no easy way out of it. I wish there was,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Oregon\u8217\'20\'19s Border Battles\par \par \uc2 from Kiran Moodley of Channel 4 News\par \par \uc2 is available\par \par \uc2 via Channel 4 News {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://youtu.be/QZhXFD50nQM"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par \uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240525ek5o001uq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
