\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 British MPs are attacking  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights. We can\u8217\'20\'19t follow the same path as the US\b0\par\par\uc2 Hilary Freeman \par \uc2 1,073 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2024\par 13:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 My own traumatic experience shows why we must push back against those who try to chip away at our freedom of choice\par \par \uc2 As the criminal justice bill stumbles through parliament this week \u8211\'20\'13 beset by delays and controversies, and picking up amendments as it goes \u8211\'20\'13 another woman, Sophie Harvey, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/article/2024/may/15/gloucestershire-teen-accused-of-abortion-told-police-she-had-stillbirth"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 is on trial}}}\uc2  for an alleged illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after taking pills to end her pregnancy when she was past the 24-week legal threshold. She was just 19 at the time. She faces a sentence of up to life in prison.\par \par \uc2 Anyone who cares about women\u8217\'20\'19s rights should be alarmed not just by this trial, but by two new amendments to the bill put forward, targeting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in England and Wales. The first, from Caroline Ansell, a Conservative MP, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-68900657#:~:text=Conservative%20Caroline%20Ansell%20has%20put,support%20of%2040%20other%20MPs."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 aims to reduce}}}\uc2  the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  limit to 22 weeks. The other, tabled by Liam Fox, another Conservative, would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-68617513"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stop women\u8217\'20\'19s choice}}}\uc2  over whether to abort a pregnancy where Down\u8217\'20\'19s syndrome looks likely, up to birth. Currently, she can choose to do so for the entirety of her pregnancy, under {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/abortion-statistics-for-england-and-wales-2021/abortion-statistics-england-and-wales-2021#:~:text=Legal%20abortions%20performed%20under%20ground,under%20ground%20E%20in%202021."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ground E of the \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Act}}}\uc2 , which allows for termination if there is \u8220\'20\'1Csubstantial risk that if the child were born it would suffer from such physical or mental abnormalities as to be seriously handicapped\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 These amendments are symptomatic of a new anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  narrative, which has crept into UK politics after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/24/scotus-roe-wade-decision-what-happens-next#:~:text=What%20happened%3F,cases%20to%20grant%20more%20rights."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturning of Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  in the United States in June 2022, prioritising the life \u8211\'20\'13 or rather existence \u8211\'20\'13 of the foetus, over the life and rights of its mother.\par \par \uc2 The rationale behind Ansell\u8217\'20\'19s amendment is data showing medical advances mean more extremely premature babies are surviving at 22 weeks rather than 24. But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/news/249374/number-extremely-premature-babies-receiving-neonatal/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 research published}}}\uc2  in November 2023 from the \uc2 University of Leicester\uc2  and \uc2 Imperial College London\uc2  shows the majority of babies born before 24 weeks still die, with only three out of 10 of babies born at 22 weeks likely to survive until they can be discharged from hospital. Those who do survive will spend many months in intensive care in hospital, and are often left with serious and lasting impairments. To whose benefit would it be to force women to give birth in such situations? As someone who has been faced with this prospect, I can tell you that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , horrible as it is, is sometimes the least bad option.\par \par \uc2 Another two amendments to this bill (from Labour MPs \uc2 Diana Johnson\uc2  and \uc2 Stella Creasy\uc2 ) aim to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bills.parliament.uk/publications/53390/documents/4174#:~:text=On%20Tuesday%2028%20November%202023,Both%20amendments%20would%20decriminalise%20abortion."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decriminalise \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 . Given that this has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.amnesty.org.uk/abortion-rights-northern-ireland-timeline#:~:text=In%20October%202019%20abortion%20decriminalised,effect%20on%2031%20March%202020."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 already happened}}}\uc2  in Northern Ireland, it should be a formality in England and Wales \u8211\'20\'13 although that\u8217\'20\'19s by no means a certainty. In the light of what has happened in the US, where more than a dozen states have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2024/us/abortion-laws-roe-v-wade.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 outlawed \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  entirely, that this is even being argued about between politicians should serve as a warning: we cannot and should not take our \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights for granted. Remember that many MPs in this government \u8211\'20\'13 in this cabinet \u8211\'20\'13 have voted to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/23/abortion-reporting-while-pregnant-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 restrict \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights}}}\uc2 , and voiced support for banning {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2012/oct/06/jeremy-hunt-12-week-abortion-limit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 very early term abortions}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As many {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bpas-campaigns.org/campaigns/roe-v-wade/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 concerned voices}}}\uc2  predicted, the momentous constitutional change in the US has emboldened our homegrown anti-choice activists here in the UK (I refuse to call them by their chosen moniker, pro-life). Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups are extremely active on social media and, despite legislation to create buffer zones around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics \u8211\'20\'13 again delayed \u8211\'20\'13 they are still {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/18/women-harassed-abortion-clinics-england-wales-buffer-zone-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 harassing women}}}\uc2  making deeply personal, often very difficult, medical decisions. They are clearly well-organised and funded. Where, we should ask, is this money coming from?\par \par \uc2 Activists and politicians are able to undermine our reproductive rights only because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains, technically, a criminal offence in the UK, under laws made in Victorian times. Unlike other healthcare issues \u8211\'20\'13 because let\u8217\'20\'19s be clear, birth is often a matter of life and death \u8211\'20\'13 it being a criminal issue means a woman can\u8217\'20\'19t just say she wants or needs a procedure; she must first get approval from two doctors and meet certain criteria. This often leads to delays, which cause distress and mean pregnancies grow more advanced (read: more complicated and requiring increasingly invasive treatment).\par \par \uc2 Like Creasy, I believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be fully decriminalised and that no woman should ever be prosecuted or imprisoned for having one. Personal experience has shown me that no woman would choose to have a late termination unless it was absolutely necessary. In 2012, after months of scans and invasive tests, I discovered the much-wanted baby I was carrying had an extremely rare and serious chromosome disorder. Were she to be born alive, which was in grave doubt, she would suffer greatly and almost certainly die within months. I was already 23 weeks pregnant.\par \par \uc2 After many painful discussions, I chose to terminate my pregnancy to spare my daughter suffering. At 24 weeks gestation, this was extremely physically and emotionally traumatic. After a procedure to stop her heart, I had to be induced, then go through a full labour and a stillbirth. My daughter\u8217\'20\'19s name was Elodie and I will never forget her, or what I went through.\par \par \uc2 Many women don\u8217\'20\'19t discover what is wrong with their pregnancy until late on. An earlier imminent legal deadline might mean they are forced to make a choice to abort before they are in possession of the full facts \u8211\'20\'13 thus leading to more abortions. By putting the rights of an unborn child before a mother\u8217\'20\'19s, we are placing the first foot on a slippery slope. Let\u8217\'20\'19s not forget that, already, women in some countries are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/10/el-salvador-woman-sentenced-prison-after-miscarriage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 imprisoned}}}\uc2  for having {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.law.cuny.edu/academics/clinical-programs/hrgj/projects/report-u-s-criminalization-of-abortion-and-pregnancy-outcomes/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 miscarriages}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The fact is, there is no need to reduce legal time limits because very few late abortions take place in the UK. In 2021, only 1% of abortions were performed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/abortion-statistics-for-england-and-wales-2021/abortion-statistics-england-and-wales-2021"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 past 20 weeks}}}\uc2 . In my opinion, the viability debate is a red herring, a distraction from what is really going on here: the chip, chip, chipping away at women\u8217\'20\'19s rights by those who want to see abortions outlawed. We must decriminalise \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  so that this cannot happen.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Hilary Freeman is a journalist and author\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Do you have an opinion on the issues raised in this article? If you would like to submit a response of up to 300 words by email to be considered for publication in our {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/letters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 letters}}}\uc2  section, please {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:mailto:guardian.letters@theguardian.com?body=Please%20include%20your%20name,%20full%20postal%20address%20and%20phone%20number%20underneath%20your%20letter.%20Letters%20are%20usually%20published%20with%20the%20author’s%20name%20and%20city/town/village.%20The%20rest%20of%20the%20information%20is%20for%20verification%20only%20and%20to%20contact%20you%20if%20your%20letter%20is%20used."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 click here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240517ek5h00109\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
