\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Louisiana moves to add  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pills to list of controlled dangerous substances\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 746 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2024\par 16:54 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 State lawmakers add amendment to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bill to reclassify mifepristone and misoprostol as Schedule IV drugs\par \par \uc2 Louisiana may soon become the first state in the country to pass a bill adding two common \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to the state\u8217\'20\'19s list of controlled dangerous substances, leading individuals who are caught with the drugs and lack authorization to potentially face years in prison.\par \par \uc2 Like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2023/nov/10/state-abortion-laws-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the rest of the US deep south}}}\uc2 , Louisiana already bans almost all abortions. But recently, when a house committee in the Republican-controlled legislature debated a bill to ban people from performing abortions on people without their consent, lawmakers added an amendment to reclassify mifepristone and misoprostol, the two drugs typically used in medication abortions, as Schedule IV drugs.\par \par \uc2 Under that amendment, a woman who obtains the drugs \u8220\'20\'1Cfor her own consumption\u8221\'20\'1D would not face penalties, but anyone who possesses them without a prescription or outside of normal medical practice could. In Louisiana, people who possess Schedule IV drugs can face up to five years in prison, while people who produce, distribute or intend to distribute such drugs can be sentenced to up to 10 years in prison. They can also face thousands of dollars in fines.\par \par \uc2 The bill would address a growing frustration among anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists: the persistent flow of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills into states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, thanks to suppliers who operate outside of the US healthcare system and who use the internet and mail to help people looking to evade \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 In March, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/25/self-managed-abortions-avoid-bans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a study found}}}\uc2  that in the six months after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, about 26,000 more Americans used pills to induce their own at-home abortions than would have done so had Roe not collapsed.\par \par \uc2 Elisa Wells, co-director of Plan C, a website that maintains a directory of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill suppliers, was unconvinced that this bill would succeed in cracking down on such suppliers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPills by mail \u8211\'20\'13 they\u8217\'20\'19re in all 50 states and authorities have not been able to prevent that from happening,\u8221\'20\'1D Wells said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe mail system is large and complex and many things are traveling through the mail that the authorities have not been able to regulate. We think it\u8217\'20\'19s too large a problem for them to effectively address.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A week after the committee on administration of criminal justice advanced the bill to criminalize possession of mifepristone and misoprostol, that same committee shot down a bill that would have carved out exceptions in Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to allow children under 16 to get the procedure if they have been the victims of rape or incest. Louisiana law currently does not permit abortions in those circumstances.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat baby [in the womb] is innocent,\u8221\'20\'1D said one Republican committee member, Dodie Horton, as she voted against the bill, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lailluminator.com/2024/05/08/rape-incest/?utm_source=substack&utm_medium=email"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the Louisiana Illuminator}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to hang on to that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Democratic representative who proposed that bill, Delisha Boyd, said that her mother gave birth to Boyd after being raped at 15. Her mother never got over the rape and died before 30, according to Boyd.\par \par \uc2 Since the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban took effect, the OB-GYN Neelima Sukhavasi told state legislators that she and colleagues had helped teenagers deliver babies after being raped.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne of these teenagers delivered a baby while clutching a teddy bear,\u8221\'20\'1D Sukhavasi said, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/US/wireStory/louisiana-lawmakers-reject-adding-exceptions-rape-incest-abortion-110010006?utm_source=substack&utm_medium=email"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to ABC News}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CAnd that\u8217\'20\'19s an image that once you see that, you can\u8217\'20\'19t unsee it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The sponsor of the bill to classify mifepristone and misoprostol as controlled substances, the Republican senator Thomas Pressly, also drew on personal experience. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/nation/2024/05/13/abortion-pills-louisiana-controlled-substance/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 His sister has said her then husband}}}\uc2  slipped her a drug to cause an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without her knowledge in 2022.\par \par \uc2 However, mifepristone and misoprostol are not only used in abortions. They can also be used to manage miscarriages, which can resemble abortions. Dealing with miscarriages has become far more fraught in the wake of Roe\u8217\'20\'19s demise, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/jan/24/us-miscarriage-laws-abortion-rights-options"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 women and doctors alike}}}\uc2  fear that women who go to hospitals for help after miscarriages could be wrongly accused of having illegal abortions and face criminal consequences.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNobody should be afraid to seek medical care when they need it,\u8221\'20\'1D Wells said. \u8220\'20\'1CThese laws that are trying to restrict access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are creating that fear and causing people to not know about their options and not trust in the options that they have available to them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240514ek5e002eb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
