\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 New laws help people in US states with bans get  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pills: \u8216\'20\'18Most people don\u8217\'20\'19t know it\u8217\'20\'19s available\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 828 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2024\par 16:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Shield laws\u8217\'20\'19 allowed medical providers to ship pills to over 40,000 people in states that forbid abortions in last five months of 2023\par \par \uc2 Over the last five months of 2023, medical providers shipped \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to over 40,000 people living in states that forbid abortions, via new \u8220\'20\'1Cshield laws\u8221\'20\'1D that protect providers who mail pills to people living under \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Researchers from #WeCount, a project by the Society of Family Planning that studies the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions following the toppling of Roe v Wade, first started tracking abortions performed through shield laws in July 2023. In its Tuesday report, the researchers measured the impact of shield laws passed in five blue states \u8211\'20\'13 Colorado, Massachusetts, New York, Vermont and Washington state \u8211\'20\'13 that protect medical providers from legal repercussions if they ship \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to people in defiance of state bans.\par \par \uc2 Between October and December 2023, an average of 5,800 monthly medication abortions in states with six-week or near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans were facilitated under such shield laws, #WeCount found in the Tuesday report, which tracks \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provision between April 2022, before Roe was overturned, and December 2023. States that permit in-person abortions but have in effect outlawed telehealth abortions, such as Arizona and North Carolina, meanwhile, saw a monthly average of almost 2,000 medication abortions provided through shield laws.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s a huge number of people who are getting abortions in a way that didn\u8217\'20\'19t exist just a few years ago,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr Ushma Upadhyay, a #WeCount co-chair, said of abortions through shield laws. Upadhyay is also a professor at \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Advancing New Standards in Reproductive Health. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s unbelievable, and it\u8217\'20\'19s a method of obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that most people in the US still don\u8217\'20\'19t even know is available.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Despite the fall of Roe, which led to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2023/nov/10/state-abortion-laws-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 across much of the US south and midwest}}}\uc2 , the number of abortions performed within the US healthcare system rose, according to the report, whose {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/19/abortion-increase-2023-data#:~:text=More%20than%201m%20abortions%20were,for%20sexual%20and%20reproductive%20health."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 findings are in line}}}\uc2  with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/24/us-abortion-rates-post-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 previous analyses}}}\uc2 . Even excluding abortions offered under shield laws, providers performed almost 86,000 monthly abortions in 2023 in states that still allow the procedure, compared with nearly 82,000 monthly abortions in 2022.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt leads to continued debate in our field. What\u8217\'20\'19s happening here? How can this be happening?\u8221\'20\'1D Upadhyay said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is really interesting, but also so important to remember that there are many people living in states with bans that aren\u8217\'20\'19t able to get their abortions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Telehealth abortions are especially on the rise. By December 2023, they accounted for 19% of all abortions nationwide.\par \par \uc2 In June, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  is expected to issue a decision on a case that could slash access to telehealth abortions. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have asked the court, which is dominated 6-3 by conservatives, to reverse measures by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  that allowed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients to receive the common \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone remotely, rather than forcing them to pick up pills in person.\par \par \uc2 The justices, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/26/us-supreme-court-abortion-pill-case-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who heard the case in March}}}\uc2 , appeared skeptical of the wide-ranging arguments made by the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups that brought the case. But if those groups prevail, clinics that offer telehealth will be affected. At least one virtual clinic {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/24/hawaii-mifepristone-abortion-pill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the Guardian}}}\uc2  it would probably shut down. Another may continue to provide abortions using a second \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, misoprostol, which is still safe but can carry more complications.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think they\u8217\'20\'19re going away, and I think they will continue offering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care,\u8221\'20\'1D Upadhyay said of providers who offer telehealth abortions. \u8220\'20\'1CI do think maybe the numbers will go down because the model will change.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19ll rock the system tremendously,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 Upadhyay is also bracing for another shock to the system: on 1 May 2024, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/may/01/florida-six-week-abortion-ban-takes-effect"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Florida banned \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  past six weeks of pregnancy, which is before many people even know they are pregnant. Because Florida used to be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/03/florida-abortion-ban-clinic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the last south-eastern state}}}\uc2  that allowed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the first trimester, people across the region often traveled there for abortions they couldn\u8217\'20\'19t get in their home state. In the 18 months after Roe fell, Florida providers performed more than 17,000 more abortions than they would have if Roe had remained the law of the land, according to #WeCount.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt remains to be seen whether the number of abortions provided under shield laws will increase as a result of Florida\u8217\'20\'19s six-week ban,\u8221\'20\'1D Upadhyay said.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 The headline, subheading and text of this article were amended on 14 May 2024. An earlier version said \u8220\'20\'1Cabout 48,000\u8221\'20\'1D people had received pills in states that forbid abortions, according to information from #WeCount, which has now said that this is incorrect and that the number is \u8220\'20\'1Cover 40,000\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240514ek5e001s5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
