\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leads \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in five key battleground states, new polls show\b0\par\par\uc2 Martin Pengelly in Washington \par \uc2 763 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2024\par 16:57 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Surveys put ex-president up in Pennsylvania, Arizona, Michigan, Georgia and Nevada as Biden campaign brushes off significance\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leads \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in five crucial battleground states less than six months out from election day, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2024/05/13/us/elections/times-siena-poll-registered-voter-crosstabs.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new polls showed}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The surveys from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , the Philadelphia Inquirer and Siena College put the former president up in Pennsylvania (three points), Arizona (seven), Michigan (seven), Georgia (10) and Nevada (12). Biden led by two points in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 All leads bar Trump\u8217\'20\'19s in Georgia and Nevada were within the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 As the poll resonated throughout the political scene, the Biden campaign issued a statement from Geoff Garin, a Democratic pollster.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe only consistency in recent public polls is inconsistency,\u8221\'20\'1D Garin said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese results need to be weighed against the 30-plus polls that show Biden up and gaining \u8211\'20\'13 which is exactly why drawing broad conclusions about the race based on results from one poll is a mistake.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump is currently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/13/trump-michael-cohen-hush-money-trial"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on trial}}}\uc2  in New York City, on 34 criminal charges arising from hush-money payments to an adult film star who claimed an affair.\par \par \uc2 That is effectively an election interference trial. The former president also faces four federal charges and 10 state charges, in Georgia, for attempted election subversion and 40 federal charges concerning his retention of classified information.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to overturn Biden\u8217\'20\'19s conclusive win in 2020 culminated in the deadly attack on Congress on 6 January 2021, by a mob he told to \u8220\'20\'1Cfight like hell\u8221\'20\'1D in his cause.\par \par \uc2 Nine deaths have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/01/05/us/politics/jan-6-capitol-deaths.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 linked}}}\uc2  to the riot including law enforcement suicides and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/usao-dc/36-months-jan-6-attack-capitol-0#:~:text=Approximately%20749%20federal%20defendants%20have,sentenced%20to%20periods%20of%20incarceration."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than}}}\uc2  1,200 people have been arrested, hundreds convicted and jailed, some for seditious conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 And yet, amid much electorate concern that at 81 Biden is too old for a second term \u8211\'20\'13 though Trump is just four years younger \u8211\'20\'13 the Times said \u8220\'20\'1Ca yearning for change and discontent over the economy and the war in Gaza among young, Black and Hispanic voters threaten to unravel the president\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic coalition\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The polls showed 20% support for Trump among Black voters, which if it held in the election would be the highest level of such support for a Republican candidate since the civil rights era.\par \par \uc2 There was better news for Biden in results culled from people who described themselves as likely to vote, with the current president leading in Michigan and narrowly behind in Wisconsin and Pennsylvania. Victories in those three states in November would probably be enough to keep Biden in the White House.\par \par \uc2 Robert F Kennedy Jr\uc2 , the third-party candidate seeking ballot access in all 50 states even while having said he once had a worm in his brain linked to cognitive problems, scored about 10% in the polls, drawing equally from both Trump and Biden.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe findings are mostly unchanged since the last series of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2023/11/07/us/elections/times-siena-battlegrounds-registered-voters.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Times/Siena polls}}}\uc2  in battleground states in November,\u8221\'20\'1D the Times said, listing factors that might be seen likely to aid Biden: the stock market gaining 25%, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s criminal trials beginning and Biden\u8217\'20\'19s campaign spending heavily in battleground states.\par \par \uc2 But voters who spoke to the paper cited cost-of-living concerns and dissatisfaction with the social and political status quo as reasons to abandon Biden for Trump.\par \par \uc2 Remarkably, the paper reported that \u8220\'20\'1Cnearly 70% of voters say that the country\u8217\'20\'19s political and economic systems need major changes \u8211\'20\'13 or even to be torn down entirely\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights also looms as a key campaign issue.\par \par \uc2 Trump has boasted of his role in appointing three rightwingers to the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 , resulting in the removal of federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in 2022 with the overturning of Roe v Wade, the ruling that guaranteed them.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have focused on the issue, taking a string of wins when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have been on the ballot, even in Republican-run states.\par \par \uc2 In the new polls, a familiar majority (64%) said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be always or mostly legal (a stance shared by 44% of Trump voters). The polls also showed voters prefer Biden to handle \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights issues by 11 points.\par \par \uc2 But nearly 20% of respondents blamed Biden more than Trump for the fall of Roe.\par \par \uc2 Garin said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe reality is that many voters are not paying close attention to the election and have not started making up their minds \u8211\'20\'13 a dynamic also reflected in today\u8217\'20\'19s poll. These voters will decide this election and only the Biden campaign is doing the work to win them over.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240513ek5d00262\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
