\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 New York attorney general sues facilities promoting \u8216\'20\'18 \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pill reversal\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 698 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2024\par 01:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Heartbeat International, affiliated with more than 2,000 facilities, aims to convince people to continue their pregnancies\par \par \uc2 The organization behind an international network of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities is misleading people with claims that abortions can be \u8220\'20\'1Creversed\u8221\'20\'1D, a lawsuit filed on Monday by Letitia James, the New York attorney general, alleges.\par \par \uc2 The organization, Heartbeat International, is affiliated with more than 2,000 facilities that aim to convince people to continue their pregnancies. In recent years, many such centers, which are often Christian and sometimes known as crisis pregnancy centers, have started to promote a controversial practice known as \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill reversal\u8221\'20\'1D, which claims that people can halt a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  midway through.\par \par \uc2 The first randomized, controlled clinical study to attempt to study this \u8220\'20\'1Creversal\u8221\'20\'1D protocol\u8217\'20\'19s effectiveness came to an abrupt stop in 2019, after three participants landed in the hospital hemorrhaging blood. The \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , the pre-eminent membership group for OB-GYNS, has said that claims about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversal are \u8220\'20\'1Cnot based on science and do not meet clinical standards\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 James\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit accuses Heartbeat International and a slew of New York-based crisis pregnancy centers of making claims about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill reversal that violate New York laws against deceptive business practices, false advertising and fraud. The lawsuit calls for the defendants to pay thousands of dollars in civil penalties.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CObtaining accurate information can be especially difficult in the context of reproductive healthcare, where opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  intentionally distort and obfuscate facts to convince, scare, shame, or guilt people facing unintended pregnancy into deciding not to obtain or complete abortions at a moment when they are particularly vulnerable,\u8221\'20\'1D alleges James\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit. When it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill reversal, the lawsuit continues: \u8220\'20\'1CDefendants\u8217\'20\'19 consumer-oriented statements distort the evidence and mislead New Yorkers to undergo this experimental \u8216\'20\'18treatment\u8217\'20\'19, believing that it has been proven safe and effective, when it has not.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Heartbeat International did not immediately reply to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Heartbeat International and the other centers named in James\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit fired a pre-emptive legal strike against the attorney general, who had alerted the organization of her plan to sue days beforehand. They filed a lawsuit that accused the Democrat of waging \u8220\'20\'1Ca politically motivated campaign against pregnancy organizations in general\u8221\'20\'1D and trampling over the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations\u8217\'20\'19 rights to free speech and free exercise of religion.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit also denied that any of the crisis pregnancy centers associated with the case had made any false or misleading statements about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill reversal.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters have spent years struggling to regulate crisis pregnancy centers and claims about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill reversal, because these centers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/feb/02/kentucky-crisis-pregnancy-center-anti-abortion-malpractices"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 operate within a kind of regulatory dead zone}}}\uc2 . Many are not licensed medical clinics, so they aren\u8217\'20\'19t subject to the kind of oversight usually imposed on such clinics (such as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics). At the same time, since the centers are usually faith-based, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/22/abortion-pill-reversal-ban-progesterone-judge-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 judges are reluctant to infringe}}}\uc2  on their religious rights.\par \par \uc2 Crisis pregnancy centers\u8217\'20\'19 promotion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill reversal works in tandem with their demonization of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to James\u8217\'20\'19 lawsuit. Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is typically induced using doses of two different drugs, now accounts for more than 60% of US abortions and has become a prime target of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists since the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  typically entails two drugs: mifepristone, which blocks progesterone, a hormone required for a pregnancy to continue, and misoprostol, which causes the uterus to empty and contract. If a woman takes mifepristone and regrets it before taking misoprostol, activists who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill reversal say that she can skip the second drug and instead take doses of progesterone to counter the effects of mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 Dr George Delgado, who helped develop the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill reversal protocol, is now one of the doctors challenging the availability of mifepristone in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/26/us-supreme-court-abortion-pill-case-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a US supreme court case}}}\uc2 . The justices are expected to issue an opinion on that case in June.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2023/04/01/health/abortion-pill-safety.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More than 100 studies}}}\uc2  have found that it is safe to use mifepristone to end a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240506ek56003h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
