\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 With six months to go, the US election is more unpredictable than ever\b0\par\par\uc2 David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 2,160 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2024\par 01:09 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Depending on the expert, either Biden or Trump is likely to pull ahead, but this election race is playing out in an unstable landscape\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou know what I hate?\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Donald Trump{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/may/01/trump-michigan-rally-new-york"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 asked in Freeland, Michigan}}}\uc2 , on Wednesday night. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen these guys get on television, they say \u8211\'20\'13 pundits, you know, the great pundits that never did a thing in their whole lives \u8211\'20\'13 \u8216\'20\'18You know, we have two very unpopular candidates. We have Biden or we have Trump. These are very unpopular.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Watched by a crowd of adoring fans in Make America Great Again (Maga) regalia, against the backdrop of a plane marked \u8220\'20\'1CTrump\u8221\'20\'1D in giant gold letters, the former US president protested a little too much: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not unpopular!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Opinion polls disagree, showing Trump with a low approval rating thanks to voter concerns over his stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , his four criminal cases and the threat he poses to constitutional democracy. Fortunately for the Republican presidential nominee, Biden has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2024/04/24/joe-bidens-approval-ratings/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 job performance troubles of his own}}}\uc2  centred on inflation, immigration and his handling of the war in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Call it the resistible force against the movable object. Six months out from one of the most consequential elections in American history, only a fool would bet with confidence on the outcome of the first presidential rematch in nearly 70 years.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hhh.umn.edu/directory/larry-jacobs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Larry Jacobs}}}\uc2 , director of the center for the study of politics and governance at the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 , said: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s almost impossible to imagine Biden winning when you start stacking up the case against him. The economy appears to be in decline with high inflation. You\u8217\'20\'19ve got signs of the Democratic coalition fraying, including the extraordinary protests and arrests of youth on college campuses, the backlash among Arab Americans with regards to Gaza.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou put that together and it\u8217\'20\'19s like, how could Biden win? And then you turn to Trump and it\u8217\'20\'19s, how could a candidate who\u8217\'20\'19s openly running on defying the will of voters win? It\u8217\'20\'19s just an incomprehensible set of choices.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Typically, an election involves two new candidates or an incumbent versus a challenger, creating plenty of scope for fresh discoveries. But Biden, 81, and 77-year-old Trump are already the two oldest men ever to occupy the White House, the subjects of countless books, newspaper articles and TV documentaries. Most voters have already made up their minds about them, or think they have.\par \par \uc2 However, a static election is playing out in an unstable landscape: British prime minister \uc2 Harold Macmillan\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s aphorism \u8220\'20\'1CEvents, dear boy, events\u8221\'20\'1D on steroids. There are the aftershocks of a global pandemic that killed more than a million Americans. The January 6 attack on the US Capitol and the supreme court decision to end the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  still reverberate. The wars in Ukraine and Gaza have provided an acid test of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s foreign policy expertise.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jan/27/frank-luntz-interview-pollster-us-uk-politics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Frank Luntz}}}\uc2 , a political consultant and pollster, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s not going to be an October surprise; every week is an October surprise. We have to get used to the predictability of the unpredictability and so it\u8217\'20\'19s very dangerous to prognosticate in this environment because things are changing so quickly.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the average of national polls, Trump leads Biden by about 1.5 percentage points, a decline for Biden of about six points from the day of the 2020 election, and Trump holds the edge in the swing states that will decide the all-important electoral college. Worryingly for the president, surveys indicate that a significant minority of Black, Latino and Asian American voters are slipping away from him.\par \par \uc2 Luntz said: \u8220\'20\'1CStrategically, Trump is being helped by the three groups that have moved towards him in the last three years: young African Americans in Georgia and North Carolina; Latino voters in Arizona and Nevada; union voters in Pennsylvania, Michigan and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBiden is weaker, but Trump has the capability to sabotage his own campaign and he won\u8217\'20\'19t even know that he\u8217\'20\'19s doing it. That\u8217\'20\'19s not something \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will do. That\u8217\'20\'19s why it\u8217\'20\'19s so dangerous to say that Trump has the advantage because, in a single day, he can ruin it for himself.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Polls suggest the economy remains the No 1 issue for voters. The perennial question \u8220\'20\'1CAre you better off than you were four years ago?\u8221\'20\'1D could win the day. While the US escaped a widely predicted recession and is growing faster than economists expected, inflation and the cost of essentials such as bread, eggs and petrol are weighing on voters.\par \par \uc2 Biden pushed through massive economic stimulus and infrastructure spending packages to boost industrial output but has received little credit from voters so far. Polls suggest that voters believe they were better off during Trump\u8217\'20\'19s presidency even though data says otherwise.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.donnabrazile.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donna Brazile}}}\uc2 , a former interim chair of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 , said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe United States is the only leading western country to come out of Covid with an economy that is not going to recession, where jobs are being created and consumer confidence is slightly rising.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut unfortunately because of the supply chain issues that are still apparent and many other factors, including greedflation, many voters are very tired of the rising costs of everyday staples, whether that\u8217\'20\'19s buying lumber or buying food on the table. It\u8217\'20\'19s unpredictable. You go to the grocery store now, you don\u8217\'20\'19t know if you need $100 or $10.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lodged deep in the national psyche, inflation could prove costly for Biden. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pughmichigan.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pamela Pugh}}}\uc2 , president of the Michigan state board of education and a candidate for the \uc2 US Congress\uc2 , has detected signs of African American voters shifting to Trump. \u8220\'20\'1CI am a Democrat but I do definitely have concern with what I do hear on the ground \u8211\'20\'13 and there is disconnect,\u8221\'20\'1D the 53-year-old said during an interview in Saginaw.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe cannot force people to feel what they don\u8217\'20\'19t feel and to say what they don\u8217\'20\'19t feel. They\u8217\'20\'19re not carrying the daily Democratic talking points. They\u8217\'20\'19re speaking what they\u8217\'20\'19re actually feeling.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDo I think that Trump would do any better for us? No. But are people looking for leadership that is going to not fight timidly, that is not going to fight scared but is going to listen to them and fight for them? That is what people are looking for.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s handling of immigration has also been criticised by both Republicans and Democrats as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/feb/07/mexico-border-explained-chart-immigration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 crossings at the US-Mexico border}}}\uc2  hit record highs. Luntz added: \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has failed badly at that and it\u8217\'20\'19s so obvious and you can\u8217\'20\'19t get away from it. We see people coming across the border. We hear about the crimes that are committed. It\u8217\'20\'19s been a shit show and arguably Trump had success with immigration; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has not.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Biden has led the response of western governments to Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine, persuading allies to punish Russia and support Kyiv. He has provided military aid to Israel in its conflict with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in Gaza while pushing for more humanitarian assistance, but has faced sharp criticism from some Democrats for not pushing harder for a ceasefire or matching his tougher rhetoric on Israel with action.\par \par \uc2 Intensifying student protests over the war in Gaza also could hurt his re-election bid as Republicans and rightwing media seek to portray the mostly peaceful demonstrations as violent and antisemitic, hoping to drive division among Democrats and promotes a sense of national chaos. Meanwhile, third-party candidates {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/20/robert-f-kennedy-jr-democratic-nomination-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Robert Kennedy Jr}}}\uc2 , Cornel West and Jill Stein could shave off further crucial votes.\par \par \uc2 Trump, more radical and extreme than in 2016 or 2020, has cast his third consecutive bid for the White House in part as retribution against perceived political enemies. He describes supporters jailed for the January 6, 2021, attack on the US Capitol as \u8220\'20\'1Chostages\u8221\'20\'1D and campaigns using increasingly dystopian rhetoric, refusing to rule out possible violence around the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 He faces 88 charges {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/28/donald-trump-investigations-criminal-charges-tracker"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in four criminal cases}}}\uc2  over efforts to subvert the 2020 election as well as unlawfully keeping classified national security documents and falsifying business records. His first trial began in New York last month, forcing him to shuttle between courtroom appearances and campaign rallies.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/04/03/us/politics/simon-rosenberg-trump-biden.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Simon Rosenberg}}}\uc2 , a Democratic strategist, said: \u8220\'20\'1CHe is far more extreme and dangerous than he was in 2020. His performance on the stump is significantly degraded and disturbing and he\u8217\'20\'19s going to be on trial for many months, which is going to further erode his strongman image and his standing with the public.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have any way to make positive news. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s central way that he\u8217\'20\'19s making positive news is by staying out of jail. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that\u8217\'20\'19s going to be very effective for the six months.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In New York, Trump has railed against the judge, violated a gag order and called the criminal charges a Democratic conspiracy designed to keep him from winning, with some of his legal challenges reaching the supreme court. The justice department denies any political interference.\par \par \uc2 If elected to another four-year term, Trump has vowed revenge on his political enemies and said he would not be a dictator except \u8220\'20\'1Con day one\u8221\'20\'1D, later calling that \u8220\'20\'1Ca joke\u8221\'20\'1D. He also wants the power to replace federal civil service workers with loyalists.\par \par \uc2 He earned opprobrium from western leaders for saying the US would not defend \uc2 Nato\uc2  members that failed to spend enough on defence and that he would encourage Russia to attack them. He also pressed congressional Republicans to stall military aid for Ukraine before reversing course.\par \par \uc2 Trump has made immigration his top domestic campaign issue, declaring he would carry out mass deportations, create holding camps, utilise the national guard and possibly federal troops, end birthright citizenship and expand a travel ban on people from certain countries. He has referred to migrants as \u8220\'20\'1Canimals\u8221\'20\'1D and has not ruled out building detention camps on US soil.\par \par \uc2 At this week\u8217\'20\'19s rally in Michigan, Karen Mantyla, 65, was wearing a T-shirt that said \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m still a Trump girl \u8211\'20\'13 I make no apologies\u8221\'20\'1D. She insisted: \u8220\'20\'1CHe\u8217\'20\'19s not a dictator, he\u8217\'20\'19s just doing what\u8217\'20\'19s right. There\u8217\'20\'19s people here that need help and they\u8217\'20\'19re letting all these illegals in and giving them everything and our poor people are suffering. They\u8217\'20\'19re being killed by terrorists coming through. It\u8217\'20\'19s ridiculous.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump claims credit for the supreme court ruling overturning Roe v Wade and said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should remain a state issue. While he has criticised some Republican-led state actions such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/may/01/florida-six-week-abortion-ban-takes-effect"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Florida\u8217\'20\'19s six-week \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  and Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s revived civil war-era ban, he said he would allow Republican-led states to track women\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancies and prosecute those who violate their state bans.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are hammering Trump over the issue and warning of the threat of a national ban. But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/articles/president-biden-trails-former-president-trump-can-he-come-back/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bill Galston}}}\uc2 , a senior fellow at the \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2  thinktank in Washington, warned: \u8220\'20\'1CSome Democrats are putting too many of their eggs in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. It\u8217\'20\'19s a lot more encouraging psychologically than focusing on weaknesses in areas like the economy and immigration, so I can understand why it\u8217\'20\'19s happening, and I hope they turn out to be right but I\u8217\'20\'19m not confident that it will. \u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump has yet to announce a vice-presidential running mate, but several possibilities have been floated. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , who ran alongside Trump in 2016 and 2020 but was targeted by Trump and his supporters amid the January 6 attack, refused to endorse him in November\u8217\'20\'19s contest. But the former president\u8217\'20\'19s base of support remains stubbornly loyal.\par \par \uc2 Bob Horny, 70, a retired builder, said: \u8220\'20\'1CHe\u8217\'20\'19s a leader. I look at all the things going on in the world that probably wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be happening right now if Trump was president \u8211\'20\'13 for example, Ukraine, Israel, $2 gas we have. Everything is unbelievable right now. People don\u8217\'20\'19t like Trump\u8217\'20\'19s personality but we\u8217\'20\'19re not voting for the pope. We\u8217\'20\'19re voting for a leader.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats remain optimistic, noting the party\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/11/08/upshot/biden-election-results-2024.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overperformance in the midterms}}}\uc2  and other recent elections, as well as Biden\u8217\'20\'19s huge fundraising advantage. Rosenberg said: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a close, competitive election, but I would much rather be us than them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COver time, as voters check in and start paying more attention, the basic contrast between Biden being a successful president leading the country through challenging times and a guy who\u8217\'20\'19s a real threat to many of the things we all hold dear will work in our favour and we\u8217\'20\'19ll win. But we have a lot of work to do and a long way to go in this election. A lot is going to happen and a lot is going to change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240505ek550015p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
