\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  invokes \u8216\'20\'18fight for our freedom\u8217\'20\'19 as Florida six-week  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban takes effect\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman in Gainesville \par \uc2 890 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 May 2024\par 21:57 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 25\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 State supreme court last month cleared the way for the \u8216\'20\'18awful\u8217\'20\'19 ban, but also allowed November ballot measure for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 A six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban went into effect on Wednesday in Florida, cutting off access to the procedure before many people know they are pregnant and leveling the south-eastern United States\u8217\'20\'19 last stronghold for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The ban went into force weeks after Florida\u8217\'20\'19s state supreme court issued a decision clearing the way for it to take effect. Strict bans now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2023/nov/10/state-abortion-laws-us#:~:text=Since%20Roe%20was%20overturned%20in,referendums%20to%20protect%20abortion%20rights."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blanket all of the American deep south}}}\uc2 , increasing the strain on the country\u8217\'20\'19s remaining clinics. The closest clinic for most Floridians past six weeks of pregnancy is now several states away in North Carolina, which outlaws \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 12 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 In a speech in Jacksonville, Florida, on Wednesday, the vice-president, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , lamented that \u8220\'20\'1Cas of this morning, 4 million women in this state woke up with fewer reproductive freedoms than they had last night.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a fight for freedom \u8211\'20\'13 the fundamental freedom to make decisions about one\u8217\'20\'19s own body and not have their government tell them what they\u8217\'20\'19re supposed to do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Harris, who has become the Biden campaign\u8217\'20\'19s foremost messenger on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , continued: \u8220\'20\'1CStarting this morning, women in Florida became subject to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban so extreme it applies before many women even know they are pregnant. Which, by the way, tells us the extremists who wrote this ban either don\u8217\'20\'19t know how a woman\u8217\'20\'19s body works or they simply don\u8217\'20\'19t care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Last year, Florida \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers performed more than 84,000 abortions, state data found \u8211\'20\'13 including more than 9,000 for out-of-state patients, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , which tracks \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. Roughly 60 % of Florida abortions {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tampabay.com/news/florida-politics/2024/04/04/florida-abortion-six-week-ban-pregnancy-planned-parenthood/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 occur after six weeks of pregnancy}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the last day before the ban took effect, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Gainesville, Florida, was trying to squeeze in as many patients as possible. The clinic had added hours throughout April, but the rush was compounded by the fact that, in addition to the impending ban, Florida requires people to have an in-person consultation at an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic at least 24 hours before they get the procedure or take \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. A patient could have arrived on Tuesday exactly six weeks into her pregnancy, but have been too late to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  given that the ban came into effect on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the female team of staffers at the clinic, Bread and Roses Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Center, had to explain these complex regulations to bewildered callers and patients over and over again, as the phones rang off the hook for hours.\par \par \uc2 Before the six-week ban, Kristin, director of Bread and Roses, said that the clinic rarely saw people before they hit six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost people don\u8217\'20\'19t know they\u8217\'20\'19re pregnant until at least six weeks,\u8221\'20\'1D Kristin said in an interview the week before the ban took effect. (Citing fears over privacy, like many other people at the clinic, Kristin asked to be identified only by her first name.) \u8220\'20\'1CWe try to get people in as quickly as possible, but sometimes we\u8217\'20\'19re one or three weeks booked out, so it\u8217\'20\'19s rare that someone is in before they\u8217\'20\'19re six weeks.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Compared with 2020, there were nearly 9,000 more abortions in Florida in 2023. Out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients accounted for almost 60% of that increase, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/monthly-abortion-provision-study"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the Guttmacher Institute}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bread and Roses used to see 20 to 30 patients daily, but in April the clinic started to see closer to 40 patients a day, Kristin said. The clinic tends to see somewhere between three to 10 out-of-state patients every day, she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs the states fell, the more increase that we\u8217\'20\'19ve been seeing from other states,\u8221\'20\'1D Kristin said.\par \par \uc2 Kristin declined to say where patients at her clinic are coming from, other than to say that they span the south-east.\par \par \uc2 In addition to its decision last month to let the six-week ban take effect, the Florida supreme court \u8211\'20\'13 which is dominated by Republican-appointed justices \u8211\'20\'13 greenlit a November ballot measure to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the state constitution. If the measure passes, the six-week ban would be deemed unconstitutional and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be legal until viability, roughly 24 weeks into pregnancy. Multiple staffers at Bread and Roses wore pins urging people to \u8220\'20\'1Cvote yes\u8221\'20\'1D on the ballot measure.\par \par \uc2 Roughly a dozen states may have residents vote directly on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in November. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters have already won ballot measures in states across the country, including in Republican bastions like Kansas and Kentucky, but in order to win in Florida, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measure will need to garner 60% of the vote.\par \par \uc2 Regardless of its outcome, the vote is months away. In the meantime, thousands of patients could be affected by the six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is going to be awful, and it\u8217\'20\'19s going to impact the whole south-east,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr K, a family medicine doctor who performs abortions at Bread and Roses. \u8220\'20\'1CI just have no idea what these patients are going to do and where they\u8217\'20\'19re gonna go.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240501ek51001e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
