\par\par \uc2 Law\par \b \uc2 Anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  states are targeting an emergency healthcare law. Will the supreme court side with them?\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,514 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 April 2024\par 19:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Justices to rule whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans should undo Emtala, the Reagan-era law requiring hospitals to treat emergency patients\par \par \uc2 One of the only universal rights to healthcare in the US is to be treated in the emergency room \u8211\'20\'13 a place where doctors are required to stabilize patients if their future health or life is in serious jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 That right, guaranteed by the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act, known across the country by healthcare professionals as Emtala, was born out of what was once a common practice called \u8220\'20\'1Cpatient dumping\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 transferring patients who could not pay from private hospitals to public counterparts, even in emergency situations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere were many reasons it was enacted,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Sara Rosenbaum\uc2 , a professor of health law at George Washington University\u8217\'20\'19s Milken Institute of Public Health, and an attorney who helped craft the Emtala law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne was because people were dumping [patients] who were uninsured, but another reason \u8211\'20\'13 and it was in the congressional record \u8211\'20\'13 was pregnant women who were being turned away,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Patients are still \u8220\'20\'1Cdumped\u8221\'20\'1D and turned away today \u8211\'20\'13 one need only look through recent hospitals found in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/refusal-emergency-care-and-patient-dumping/2009-01"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 violation of Emtala}}}\uc2  for those cases \u8211\'20\'13 but few instances compare to the galling treatment of pregnant women before the law\u8217\'20\'19s passage.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI remember a young woman in labor who was sent to Parkland [Memorial hospital in Dallas] from another hospital \u8211\'20\'13 a religiously affiliated hospital,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr Ron J Anderson said in an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aha.org/system/files/2018-02/ron-anderson-md-oral-history.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 oral history}}}\uc2  recorded in 2014. Anderson was in charge of the hospital\u8217\'20\'19s emergency room at the time. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen she told them that she was so glad that they would still see her even though her husband had lost his job, they pushed her legs together, started an IV, and sent her to Parkland.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The woman was \u8220\'20\'1Ccrowning\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 in an advanced stage of labor \u8211\'20\'13 and ultimately delivered in Parkland\u8217\'20\'19s hallway. Anderson was so angry he went to the other hospital and challenged the doctor to \u8220\'20\'1Cgo outside\u8221\'20\'1D, a euphemism for a fight. \u8220\'20\'1CI was pretty hot-headed about it,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 This is about a 40-year-old law that is the only universal guarantee of healthcare Americans have\par \par \uc2 Sara Rosenbaum\par \par \uc2 Pregnant women were far from the only group being dumped by private hospitals. In the same oral history, Anderson recounts a cab driver who arrived at the emergency room with a man who had been stabbed, the knife moving up and down as he breathed, with an IV from another hospital hanging on the cab\u8217\'20\'19s coat hook.\par \par \uc2 In the coming week, the supreme court will consider whether this law \u8211\'20\'13 passed in 1986 to protect pregnant women, their future fertility and their children \u8211\'20\'13 should be undone by the near-total state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans which conflict directly with its provisions. The case is a direct consequence of Republican-led states\u8217\'20\'19 quest for total control over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation.\par \par \uc2 To understand how the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement came to target Emtala, it is worth revisiting how the law came to be in America\u8217\'20\'19s byzantine system of largely private healthcare.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is about a 40-year-old law that is the only universal guarantee of healthcare Americans have,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rosenbaum. If the supreme court finds in favor of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states, \u8220\'20\'1CWe could find ourselves in a world where some state running some kind of culture war agenda has carved up emergency room access.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Signed into law by Ronald Reagan, Emtala forced hospitals to treat these emergency patients or risk losing funding from Medicare. Medicare, the federal health insurance program for the elderly, and its sister program Medicaid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aha.org/system/files/media/file/2022/05/fact-sheet-majority-hospital-payments-dependent-on-medicare-or-medicaid-congress-continues-to-cut-hospital-reimbursements-for-medicare.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 routinely account}}}\uc2  for more than half of hospitals\u8217\'20\'19 total revenue.\par \par \uc2 A collision of federal and state law\par \par \uc2 But Emtala, and all its protections for American patients, were put on a crash course with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans almost immediately after the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade in 2022.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe decision came down at 10.02 and at 10.03 I was calling my daughter to tell any friend in a pre-viability [\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban] state not to get pregnant,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rosenbaum. \u8220\'20\'1CThey should not be pregnant in those states, whether it was a pregnancy-related emergency or just an emergency,\u8221\'20\'1D as those pregnant women, said Rosenbaum, \u8220\'20\'1Cwould not get proper emergency care\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The conflict between \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and Emtala\u8217\'20\'19s requirements make treating pregnant patients a fraught proposition.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court will hear oral arguments on Wednesday in a case called Idaho v United States. The case was brought after Idaho imposed a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that allowed doctors to perform an emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if a pregnant patient was on the brink of death.\par \par \uc2 That law is in direct conflict with Emtala, which requires doctors to stabilize emergency patients so they won\u8217\'20\'19t face severe health consequences \u8211\'20\'13 a radically lower bar for intervention than Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s. Shortly after Roe was overturned, the Biden administration issued a guidance stating that the federal law pre-empts state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, ultimately suing Idaho over its ban.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the state of Idaho argued in its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/23/23A470/290151/20231120154722276_2023-11-20%20Motion%20with%20Appendix.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 petition to the supreme court}}}\uc2  that Emtala was silent on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that the law was meant only to ensure hospitals did not turn away uninsured patients. It also argued it was the state\u8217\'20\'19s right to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to only be performed to save the life of a mother, based on \u8220\'20\'1Cmore than 100 years\u8221\'20\'1D of legislation including an 1864 territorial law enacted before Idaho was a state.\par \par \uc2 Already, the chilling effect of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans on treatment is clear from a spike in harrowing complaints about pregnant people turned away from hospital emergency rooms. A Missouri woman named Mylissa Farmer was denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in both Mississippi and Kansas, in spite of the fact that her 17-week-old fetus would not survive; she risked severe infection and the loss of her uterus. She was forced to travel to Illinois for an emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Farmer\u8217\'20\'19s case became one of the first investigations of an Emtala violation in the era of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was dehumanizing. It was terrifying. It was horrible not to get the care to save your life,\u8221\'20\'1D Farmer, who lives in Joplin, Missouri, told the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/emergency-abortion-law-hospitals-kansas-missouri-emtala-2f993d2869fa801921d7e56e95787567"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Associated Press}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Texas, a woman miscarried in the lobby of a hospital when staff refused to admit her. In North Carolina, another state with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, a woman gave birth in a car after a hospital refused her an ultrasound, according to reporting by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/US/wireStory/emergency-rooms-refused-treat-pregnant-women-leaving-miscarry-109409806"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ABC News}}}\uc2 . The newborn later died.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou see a pregnant person walk through the door in Idaho or Texas, or any of these states that have actively interfered with Emtala now, and it\u8217\'20\'19s like potential prison time if you do anything\u8221\'20\'1D as a doctor, Rosenbaum said.\par \par \uc2 Ironically, Texas, which has also challenged the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s guidance on Emtala, helped assure its passage in the 1980s. The state first passed protections against {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.healthaffairs.org/content/forefront/emtala-pregnancy-protections-versus-state-abortion-bans-supreme-court-decide"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cpatient dumping\u8221\'20\'1D in 1985}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat this would mean is that, in many red states in America, the only exception to their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions or bans is for the life of the mother \u8211\'20\'13 not their health,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lawrence Gostin, a health law expert and distinguished professor at Georgetown Law. \u8220\'20\'1CSo, as a result, there\u8217\'20\'19s some women who have to sit in parking lots or in waiting rooms until their condition gets so serious that they could die.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe values at stake here are not just ideological \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate material,\u8221\'20\'1D Gostin said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis affects people\u8217\'20\'19s lives and the right to go to a competent doctor when they\u8217\'20\'19re in dire urgent needs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Doctors have argued that exceptions to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/11/14/texas-abortion-laws-lawsuit/#:~:text=The%20new%20plaintiffs%2C%20like%20the,expected%20to%20survive%20after%20birth."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 are completely unworkable}}}\uc2 , particularly those that only provide for doctors to save a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen is it certain she will die but for medical intervention?\u8221\'20\'1D asks an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acog.org/-/media/project/acog/acogorg/files/advocacy/amicus-briefs/2024/emtala-0328-for-filing.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 amicus or friend of the court brief}}}\uc2  by three of the US\u8217\'20\'19s largest and most esteemed professional associations of doctors \u8211\'20\'13 the \uc2 American College of Emergency Physicians\uc2 , the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , and the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CHow many blood units does she have to lose? One? Two? Five?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Emergency medicine doctors see pregnant patients on \u8220\'20\'1Cvirtually every shift\u8221\'20\'1D, the brief says, and complications arising from pregnancy can be severe and threaten life and limb. Without treatment, complications from pregnancy can result in the loss of a uterus, \u8220\'20\'1Cseizures, stroke, vital organ damage failure and death\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s more, the number of abortions happening in hospital emergency rooms is diminishingly small, according to experts and doctors \u8211\'20\'13 perhaps only as many as a few dozen out of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/news-release/2024/number-abortions-united-states-likely-be-higher-2023-2020"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 roughly 1 million}}}\uc2  abortions a year in the US, according to Rosenbaum.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe hope coming out of the oral argument and the briefing is that there are enough justices who will understand \u8211\'20\'13 including a couple justices who voted [to overturn Roe v Wade] \u8211\'20\'13 this is an exceptionally vital and narrow statute,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rosenbaum.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This article was amended on 26 April 2024 to correct that Joplin is a city in Missouri, not Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240421ek4l001bb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
