\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  U-turn points to Republican break from anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  movement\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 933 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 April 2024\par 12:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even in states as red as Kentucky and Kansas grows, the GOP appears to be breaking ranks with the hardliners\par \par \uc2 In the two years since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, the 50-year-long partnership between Republicans and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists has steadily fractured as political backlash to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans has mounted. But perhaps the biggest crack emerged on Wednesday, when Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s Republican-controlled state legislature moved to repeal the state\u8217\'20\'19s 1864 near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 After days of national outcry over an Arizona supreme court ruling that upheld the 1864 ban, passed before Arizona became a state, three Republicans in the state house {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/24/arizona-repeal-abortion-ban-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 broke ranks to vote with Democrats}}}\uc2  to support a repeal. Last week, two Republicans in the state senate also voted with Democrats to advance a similar repeal. As long as those Republican votes hold firm, Democrats will likely send the repeal to Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic governor in May.\par \par \uc2 Before the Wednesday vote, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists spent days {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/24/arizona-abortion-1864-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lobbying Republicans}}}\uc2  to stand against the repeal. Yet despite their obvious displeasure afterward, some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have held their fire. Rather than immediately threatening to primary the Republicans who defected from their cause, some advocates have struck a relatively muted tone, grudgingly urging lawmakers to at least back a 15-week ban, which is currently in effect in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Before the fall of Roe, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists were becoming increasingly hardline, often pressuring the GOP to drop candidates who lacked anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bona fides. But after Wednesday, rather than directing their ire at the Republicans who are making the repeal possible, they have largely blamed the usual suspects: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates. \u8220\'20\'1CThe most protective pro-life law in the country is poised to fall to the appetites of pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists,\u8221\'20\'1D Cathi Herrod, president of the powerful Center for Arizona Policy, said in a statement, adding that she supported efforts to \u8220\'20\'1Cdefend the state\u8217\'20\'19s 15-week law if the pre-Roe law was repealed\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of SBA Pro-Life America, joined Herrod in defending the 15-week ban. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes are fearful of a potential November ballot measure to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CKari Lake and all GOP candidates and elected officials must bring clarity to Arizona voters by campaigning vigorously in support of Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s 15-week protection with exceptions and in opposition to the extreme no-limits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment,\u8221\'20\'1D Dannenfelser said in a statement of her own, which also mourned the probable fall of the 1864 law. Kari Lake, running to represent Arizona in the US senate, has in recent days, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/apr/11/kari-lake-arizona-abortion-reversal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 repeatedly flip-flopped}}}\uc2  on her position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ingrid Duran, state legislative director for the National Right to Life, another leading anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization, said she was \u8220\'20\'1Csurprised\u8221\'20\'1D by the Arizona Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 change of heart. But she said she believes the movement\u8217\'20\'19s overall relationship with the GOP can recover.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI feel like this pivot has been solely based on some of the fear mongering that has been taking place from our opposition, and the people are responding to that, and some are caving into this pressure,\u8221\'20\'1D Duran said in an interview. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have always been a movement that has been there for the long haul. And even with a setback like this, I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that that should deter us from trying to save as many lives as possible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Next year, Duran said, her organization may try to once again pursue some kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in Arizona, such as a six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CArizona has some of the most radical anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists around,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jennifer Holland, a US history professor at the \uc2 University of Oklahoma\uc2  and the author of Tiny You: A Western History of the Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Movement. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I do think that now, after Roe\u8217\'20\'19s been overturned, the movement is fragmenting a little bit more in terms of strategy, partly because the political landscape is just so different.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Between 1973, when the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  decided Roe, and 2022, when the court overturned it, the GOP and the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement grew to operate in lock-step. The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement handed Republicans ardent voters; in exchange, Republicans in state legislatures voted in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 For the GOP, those votes were a cheap way to build a base, since the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that Roe established blocked many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions from going into effect and thus shielded Republicans from political fallout.\par \par \uc2 But now that Roe is gone, the GOP has struggled to distance itself from its own policies. Outrage over Roe\u8217\'20\'19s death {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/11/abortion-midterm-elections-republicans-women-voters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cost Republicans votes}}}\uc2  in the 2022 midterms. A flurry of ballot measures to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have succeeded even in states as red as Kansas and Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, failures in state races and legislatures will amp up anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists\u8217\'20\'19 focus on the presidential race, and the possibility for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that could come with a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  victory, according to Holland.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe more that they lose in places like Arizona and West Virginia and Kansas, I think that there\u8217\'20\'19s even more pressure for national bans,\u8221\'20\'1D Holland said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis movement has this moral goal. It is neither here nor there whether it is democratically reached or democratically supported. The goal is there should be no \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it\u8217\'20\'19s murder. So if that principle loses in more and more states, then the only solutions that achieve that goal are federal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240426ek4q001jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
