\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18How sick do they have to get?\u8217\'20\'19 Doctors brace for \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  hearing on emergency abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,616 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 April 2024\par 13:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As states ban abortions, a 1986 federal law requires hospitals that receive Medicare dollars to stabilize patients in a medical emergency, creating a catch-22 for care providers\par \par \uc2 Dr Lauren Miller used to cry every day on her way to work.\par \par \uc2 A fetal maternal medicine specialist in Idaho, Miller despaired over the possibility she might be forced to tell patients she could not help them. Idaho has one of the strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the nation, which means Miller could only perform abortions to save a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life \u8211\'20\'13 and many patients, even those facing medical emergencies with potentially deadly consequences, were not yet sick enough to qualify.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll I could say is: \u8216\'20\'18We have to get them a transport out of state,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt just breaks my heart that I knew them and had a relationship with them and couldn\u8217\'20\'19t offer them the same care that I could have given them a year ago\u8221\'20\'1D, before Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s ban went into effect.\par \par \uc2 One law, however, briefly allowed Miller to provide abortions in emergencies, Miller said: a 1986 federal law, almost unknown outside of the medical field, known as the Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act, or Emtala. That law requires hospitals that receive Medicare dollars to stabilize any patients who may show up at their doors in the midst of a medical emergency \u8211\'20\'13 a potentially lower threshold than what is allowed under Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s ban, which allows for abortions if a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life is in jeopardy. If a hospital can\u8217\'20\'19t stabilize a patient, according to Emtala, the hospital must transport the patient to a facility that can.\par \par \uc2 But now even Emtala, the subject of a case that will be heard by the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  this week, may be permanently pulled out from under Miller and other doctors in her situation who are struggling to understand what role they can play in treating patients in crisis.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after the court overturned Roe v Wade in 2022, ushering in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans throughout the country, the Biden administration declared that Emtala applies to people who may need emergency abortions and pre-empts any bans that state otherwise. It later filed a lawsuit against Idaho, arguing that its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban violates Emtala and forces doctors into an untenable catch-22: if they follow Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s law, they may violate a federal one.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHow high a risk does a patient have to take, or how sick do they have to get, before it\u8217\'20\'19s OK to actually give them the medical care that they need?\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Stacy Seyb, a maternal fetal medicine specialist in Idaho. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is not good medicine to let them get extremely sick.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the nation\u8217\'20\'19s highest court will hear arguments in the case \u8211\'20\'13 and the conservative majority\u8217\'20\'19s opinion could have vast consequences for states across the country.\par \par \uc2 Six other states have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans on the books that look a lot like Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s; rather than permitting abortions in cases where a patient\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Chealth\u8221\'20\'1D is at risk, these states \u8211\'20\'13 which include South Dakota, Mississippi, Oklahoma and Arizona \u8211\'20\'13 only allow abortions to save a patient\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Clife\u8221\'20\'1D. In the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s view, these bans could conflict with Emtala.\par \par \uc2 Dr Kristin Lyerly used to work as an OB-GYN in Wisconsin, which also has a law on the books that only permits life-saving abortions and has been singled out by the Biden administration for potentially conflicting with Emtala. Today, Lyerly works in Minnesota. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t feel safe practicing in Wisconsin right now,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains available at some clinics in Wisconsin as the ban, which dates back to 1849, is being litigated \u8211\'20\'13 but\par \par \uc2 Lyerly said some Wisconsin hospitals had not provided the procedure since Roe fell.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 A federal court in Idaho initially agreed that Emtala protected doctors\u8217\'20\'19 ability to perform emergency abortions, but in January, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ruled to let Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s full ban take effect, in effect overriding the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s view of Emtala. Idaho, which is being represented by the Christian powerhouse law firm the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , has argued in court papers that Emtala has nothing to do with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it does not authorize doctors to perform procedures that are otherwise illegal.\par \par \uc2 In 2023, before the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ruling, just one woman was taken out of state for care for maternal complications, said Dr Jim Souza, the chief physician executive for an Idaho hospital system, in a call with reporters. But since January, he said, six have had to be transported out of Idaho.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe can anticipate up to 20 patients needing out-of-state care this year alone,\u8221\'20\'1D Souza said. \u8220\'20\'1CPutting somebody in a whirly bird and flying them to another state creates an obvious delay in care that puts the patient\u8217\'20\'19s health and life at risk. If she is in transit and begins exsanguinating \u8211\'20\'13 hemorrhaging very quickly \u8211\'20\'13 the resources you have are no longer the resources of a tertiary care center. They\u8217\'20\'19re the resources of a helicopter.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 How high a risk does a patient have to take, or how sick do they have to get, before it\u8217\'20\'19s okay to actually give them the medical care that they need?\par \par \uc2 Dr Stacy Seyb\par \par \uc2 One brief filed in the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  case by the organization \uc2 Physicians for Human Rights\uc2  detailed how an Oregon OB-GYN took care of a patient who had been transferred in from Idaho. The woman had developed pre-eclampsia and needed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but, unable to get one in Idaho, had begun to bleed so much that she developed anemia. Her kidneys also started to fail.\par \par \uc2 The kind of cases that may fall under Emtala\u8217\'20\'19s purview usually involve patients who want to be parents but face some complication that makes proceeding with a pregnancy dangerous, said Dr Sara Thomson, an OB-GYN in Idaho.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf your water breaks really early in the pregnancy, that\u8217\'20\'19s already such a horrible, heartbreaking conversation to have, but it is so much harder now,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIn addition to having to have that conversation, we then have to navigate: is this patient sick enough to offer her delivery in our state? Or do we have to further traumatize her by talking to her about leaving the state or telling a patient you\u8217\'20\'19re sick but not sick enough to have treatment right now?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The doctors who spoke to the Guardian agreed: Emtala is not enough to fix the medical quandaries unleashed by the fall of Roe and the expansion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. But, Thomson said: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s certainly better than what we have.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Many doctors have refused to work under these conditions, moving out of states that, they say, stop them from being able to treat their patients.\par \par \uc2 In January 2023, Dr Leilah Zahedi-Spung left her dream job as a maternal fetal medicine specialist in Tennessee over that state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. At the time, Tennessee and Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s restrictions on the procedure were very similar: rather than carving out an explicit exception for patients facing medical emergencies, the laws said that doctors who were prosecuted for performing medically necessary abortions could claim an \u8220\'20\'1Caffirmative defense\u8221\'20\'1D in court \u8211\'20\'13 a standard that critics have said amounts to \u8220\'20\'1Cguilty until proven innocent\u8221\'20\'1D. Both Tennessee and Idaho have since stripped the \u8220\'20\'1Caffirmative defense\u8221\'20\'1D provisions from their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 Every time Zahedi-Spung had to send a patient out of Tennessee, she told the hospital where she worked that she felt like she was violating Emtala. She declined to say whether she ever performed any medically necessary post-Roe abortions in Tennessee, but she did hire a criminal defense attorney to protect herself.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was never gonna let anyone die in front of me,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Zahedi-Spung now practices in Colorado, where she regularly sees patients fleeing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans from states as close as Idaho and as far away as Florida. Zahedi-Spung also works with Miller, who decided to relocate to Colorado last year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI just could not comply with a law that could easily leave a mother dead,\u8221\'20\'1D Miller said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was too counter to my own moral and professional codes. I refuse to be complicit in such reproductive injustices.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I just could not comply with a law that could easily leave a mother dead. I refuse to be complicit in such reproductive injustices\par \par \uc2 Dr Lauren Miller\par \par \uc2 Thomson wants to stay in Idaho, but she recently updated her r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 for the first time in a decade, in case she decides to seek a new job. Seyb said that he plans to stay in Idaho, but that the uncertainty around the future of the ban may hasten his retirement.\par \par \uc2 A 2023 survey of more than 100 Idaho doctors, all of whom have had their practice affected by the state\u8217\'20\'19s ban, found that about two-thirds were considering moving out of state in the next year. Of that share, 93% blamed the ban.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe problem is that you\u8217\'20\'19re pitting patients and physicians against each other,\u8221\'20\'1D Zahedi-Spung said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf the physician provides the care the patient needs, then the physician is in harm\u8217\'20\'19s way. And if the physician doesn\u8217\'20\'19t provide the care the patient needs, the patient is in harm\u8217\'20\'19s way. But neither of them can ever be safe at the same time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This subheading of this article was amended on 23 April 2024 to clarify that a 1986 federal law requires hospitals that receive Medicare dollars, not Medicaid dollars as an earlier version said, to stabilize patients in a medical emergency.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240423ek4n001bg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
