\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s at stake in emergency  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  care case before \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 ?\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza in New York \par \uc2 730 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2024\par 21:54 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The justices must decide whether Idaho doctors can terminate a pregnancy to save a woman\u8217\'20\'19s health or only if her life is at risk\par \par \uc2 The supreme court heard its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2024/apr/24/us-supreme-court-abortion-rights-emtala"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 second \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights case }}}\uc2  of the term on Wednesday, this one focused on how states can regulate emergency abortions \u8211\'20\'13 exceedingly rare procedures that often save a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life or her future fertility.\par \par \uc2 The case may seem technical because it focuses on a small subset of emergency abortions and federal law that governs emergency room care.\par \par \uc2 However, if the court finds in favor of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states, it could upend emergency care for millions of pregnant women and undermine the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s ability to regulate emergency medicine.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what you need to know about the law\par \par \uc2 The Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act (Emtala) is a federal law that requires doctors and hospitals to provide stabilizing treatment to patients facing serious threats to life and limb, regardless of their ability to pay, or lose federal funding.\par \par \uc2 The law grew out of \u8220\'20\'1Cpatient dumping\u8221\'20\'1D in the 1980s, when private hospitals transferred patients who could not pay, including women in active labor, to public counterparts.\par \par \uc2 Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban only allows doctors to intervene in near-death cases\par \par \uc2 Emtala requires doctors to stabilize patients if they face a threat to life or limb. If a patient\u8217\'20\'19s health could be seriously impaired in the future \u8211\'20\'13 for example, if a pregnant woman could lose her uterus unless she receives an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 an emergency room must make an effort to treat that threat to health.\par \par \uc2 This requirement directly conflicts with Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which only allows doctors to terminate a pregnancy to prevent death. Otherwise, doctors could face prosecution and loss of their medical license.\par \par \uc2 So, how can healthcare providers comply with Emtala and Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s law? Lawyers for Idaho say there is no conflict. Because doctors must work within the bounds of state licensing boards, Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s attorneys said they could comply with Emtala by simply following Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s imminent death standard.\par \par \uc2 Female justices appeared especially skeptical. Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , a member of the conservative wing, asked Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s deputy solicitor general, Joshua Turner, if a doctor would face criminal charges if they treated a woman who could lose her uterus but not die. Not if a doctor was \u8220\'20\'1Cexercising their medical judgment to determine life-saving care was necessary\u8221\'20\'1D, Turner said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat if the prosecutor thought differently?\u8221\'20\'1D asked Coney Barrett.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat is the nature of prosecutorial discretion,\u8221\'20\'1D said Turner.\par \par \uc2 The federal government says Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s law is dangerous\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for the federal government vehemently disagreed and Turner\u8217\'20\'19s assurances that doctors could exercise medical judgment do not square with reality, said the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s solicitor general, \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIdaho has directly interfered with the ability of hospitals to accept these federal funds and fulfill Congress\u8217\'20\'19s desire that no matter where you are in this country you can go to an ER and be stabilized,\u8221\'20\'1D said Prelogar.\par \par \uc2 Doctors in Idaho are so scared of treating pregnant patients that the state\u8217\'20\'19s largest emergency care department is regularly airlifting women to Utah, Prelogar said. Utah also has an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, but it makes an exception for women\u8217\'20\'19s health, and therefore does not conflict with Emtala. At least six more states have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that do not make an exception for women\u8217\'20\'19s health, Prelogar said, though not all are in effect.\par \par \uc2 Herein lies some of this case\u8217\'20\'19s most pernicious implications \u8211\'20\'13 if doctors do not feel safe, then treating any pregnant patient in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban state becomes a high-risk proposition. It means women will be and already are being turned away from emergency rooms \u8211\'20\'13 endangering one of the populations Congress sought to protect.\par \par \uc2 The court appears divided\par \par \uc2 Whether the court will side with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states or the federal government is unclear. The court\u8217\'20\'19s three liberals are almost certain to vote against anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states. At least one conservative, Coney Barrett, also appeared skeptical.\par \par \uc2 The conservative justices \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  appeared supportive of Idaho. The votes of three more conservative justices are less clear. Liberals will need at least two conservatives to join them to protect emergency care as Americans know it today.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240424ek4o003h4\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 atleast5(abortion) OR atleast3("reproductive justice")\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Date\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/2021 to 06/30/2026\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Source\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 The Guardian (U.K.)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\